1
00:01:20,781 --> 00:01:24,183
- And did you go alone?
- No.

2
00:01:24,618 --> 00:01:26,211
Thank you.

3
00:01:27,020 --> 00:01:29,956
So old Mark here just all of a
sudden looks up at me and says,

4
00:01:30,123 --> 00:01:31,887
"Walnut Grove Textiles."

5
00:01:32,059 --> 00:01:34,979
Next thing I know, we're buying half
a million shares at 13 and a quarter.

6
00:01:35,128 --> 00:01:38,098
- Thirteen and three-eights.
- I almost had him committed.

7
00:01:38,265 --> 00:01:40,700
I mean, who the hell has even
heard of Walnut Grove Textiles?

8
00:01:40,867 --> 00:01:41,960
But one week later,

9
00:01:42,135 --> 00:01:43,695
guess who gets bought
out by Value-Rite.

10
00:01:43,837 --> 00:01:47,433
Tripled our money, just
like that. The guy's a genius.

11
00:01:47,608 --> 00:01:50,840
Or maybe he just has
access to the right information.

12
00:01:51,311 --> 00:01:53,906
There's no need to break the
law to be a player in this business.

13
00:01:54,081 --> 00:01:55,310
The information's out there.

14
00:01:55,482 --> 00:01:57,110
Who's making money,
who's losing money,

15
00:01:57,284 --> 00:01:59,344
who's smart, who's on the make.

16
00:01:59,519 --> 00:02:02,580
You just have to
know how to digest it.

17
00:02:02,756 --> 00:02:05,157
Actually, it's those
glasses he wears.

18
00:02:05,325 --> 00:02:08,295
They make it so he can read
between the lines. Don't they, Mark?

19
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:10,692
Tell you what, let's trade.

20
00:02:10,864 --> 00:02:13,390
Your glasses for my wife here.

21
00:02:13,567 --> 00:02:16,867
- That's my wife, Cameron.
- Oops. Sorry about that, John. Heh.

22
00:02:17,037 --> 00:02:19,802
Now, what did I do with my wife?

23
00:02:19,973 --> 00:02:21,635
I believe you divorced her.

24
00:02:22,509 --> 00:02:24,671
Oh. So I did.

25
00:02:24,845 --> 00:02:28,373
In that case, quick. Will you marry
me? I'll buy you your very own company.

26
00:02:29,416 --> 00:02:30,907
You like insurance?

27
00:02:31,084 --> 00:02:33,918
I'll buy you this guy's
company. Ashfield Life.

28
00:02:34,087 --> 00:02:36,283
- It's doing very well.
- I know.

29
00:02:36,456 --> 00:02:38,584
- He's my husband.
- Oh.

30
00:02:38,759 --> 00:02:40,091
I need another drink.

31
00:02:41,428 --> 00:02:43,363
- What was that?
- I don't know.

32
00:02:52,439 --> 00:02:54,499
Ah, John throws a heck
of a party, doesn't he?

33
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:56,644
- Mm-hm.
- Anything look interesting?

34
00:02:56,810 --> 00:02:58,745
Stuffed mushrooms look good.

35
00:02:58,912 --> 00:03:01,746
So does Ashfield
Life. All kidding aside.

36
00:03:01,915 --> 00:03:04,350
So when are you gonna
make me a rich man?

37
00:03:04,518 --> 00:03:06,783
Soon as I'm sure I'll
wind up even richer.

38
00:04:06,713 --> 00:04:08,614
<i>91 1 emergency.</i>

39
00:04:08,782 --> 00:04:11,251
<i>275... Go ahead.</i>

40
00:04:11,418 --> 00:04:13,478
So it was you who managed...

41
00:04:15,288 --> 00:04:16,756
Oh, God, he's dead, isn't he?

42
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:19,017
Well, they're doing
everything they can, sir.

43
00:04:19,192 --> 00:04:20,792
Why don't you tell me
what happened here?

44
00:04:20,961 --> 00:04:22,486
I don't know, I was
just driving along.

45
00:04:22,662 --> 00:04:24,756
Next thing I knew, he
was right in front of me.

46
00:04:24,931 --> 00:04:26,957
Sir, have you been drinking?

47
00:04:27,734 --> 00:04:30,329
Glass of champagne,
maybe two. That's all.

48
00:04:30,504 --> 00:04:34,407
Okay, I have to ask you to
step over here with me, please.

49
00:04:38,612 --> 00:04:40,945
Now, I need you to stand
with your feet together

50
00:04:41,114 --> 00:04:42,138
and close your eyes.

51
00:04:42,315 --> 00:04:44,011
Come on, I'm not drunk.

52
00:04:44,184 --> 00:04:46,779
Sir, are you refusing
to cooperate?

53
00:04:49,222 --> 00:04:53,683
Now, tilt your head back,
place your arms out to the side,

54
00:04:53,860 --> 00:04:55,226
and with your right forefinger,

55
00:04:55,395 --> 00:04:56,995
reach out and touch
the tip of your nose.

56
00:04:57,164 --> 00:04:58,564
Can you do that for me?

57
00:05:08,508 --> 00:05:10,170
My client may have
had some champagne

58
00:05:10,343 --> 00:05:13,211
before he got behind the wheel
of his car that night, Your Honor,

59
00:05:13,380 --> 00:05:17,215
and, tragically, he may have struck
and killed someone on his way home.

60
00:05:17,384 --> 00:05:19,744
But I'm going to show you in
the course of these proceedings

61
00:05:19,886 --> 00:05:23,186
that those two facts are
not necessarily related.

62
00:05:23,356 --> 00:05:25,791
I'm going to show you that
the charge of vehicular homicide

63
00:05:25,959 --> 00:05:26,983
is unwarranted,

64
00:05:27,160 --> 00:05:29,095
that Mr. Ivers does
not deserve to go to jail,

65
00:05:29,262 --> 00:05:31,788
for I am going to show
that hitting that poor man

66
00:05:31,965 --> 00:05:35,367
was something no one, drunk
or sober, could've avoided.

67
00:05:35,535 --> 00:05:38,300
That man died not because
my client had been drinking,

68
00:05:38,471 --> 00:05:40,440
but because he had the
misfortune of wandering out

69
00:05:40,607 --> 00:05:42,200
onto a dark, winding road,

70
00:05:42,375 --> 00:05:43,468
and being struck by a car

71
00:05:43,643 --> 00:05:45,874
that any one of us here
could've been driving.

72
00:05:46,046 --> 00:05:47,878
Thank you, Your Honor.

73
00:05:48,081 --> 00:05:49,379
Thank you, Mr. Browning.

74
00:05:49,549 --> 00:05:52,041
Is the state ready to
call its first witness?

75
00:05:52,219 --> 00:05:53,243
Yes, Your Honor.

76
00:05:53,420 --> 00:05:56,254
We call Sergeant Travis
Farrow to the stand.

77
00:05:57,290 --> 00:06:00,351
<i>Goodbye my Coney Island, baby</i>

78
00:06:00,527 --> 00:06:02,689
<i>Farewell my own true love, true</i>

79
00:06:02,863 --> 00:06:05,423
Not bad, huh? Ha-ha-ha.

80
00:06:05,599 --> 00:06:08,433
Ah. I don't know.

81
00:06:08,668 --> 00:06:11,536
Of course, I got it. It's
upstairs in my closet.

82
00:06:11,705 --> 00:06:14,504
Yeah. Okay, count me in.

83
00:06:14,674 --> 00:06:16,768
All right, I'll see you then.

84
00:06:17,043 --> 00:06:18,272
Who was that?

85
00:06:18,445 --> 00:06:19,936
That's an old
friend from school.

86
00:06:20,113 --> 00:06:23,277
He's drumming up some people to
come to our class reunion. Fortieth.

87
00:06:23,450 --> 00:06:25,385
Well, what was all that
"Coney Island, baby... "?

88
00:06:25,552 --> 00:06:27,544
I used to be a member
of a barbershop quartet.

89
00:06:27,721 --> 00:06:29,314
- Yeah?
- Yeah, yeah.

90
00:06:29,489 --> 00:06:31,754
There's me and Gus
and George and Chuck.

91
00:06:31,925 --> 00:06:33,416
- Fairview Fair Tones.
- Yeah.

92
00:06:33,593 --> 00:06:35,494
Fairview was the
name of our dormitory.

93
00:06:35,662 --> 00:06:38,131
- Are they all coming to the reunion?
- Yeah, yeah.

94
00:06:38,298 --> 00:06:40,658
We're gonna wear the outfits
we used to wear and everything.

95
00:06:40,800 --> 00:06:43,497
- Ha, ha. That's great.
- Yeah. Yeah.

96
00:06:43,670 --> 00:06:46,606
You know, we have to
have this filed by 5:00.

97
00:06:46,773 --> 00:06:48,435
Oh, yeah, yeah. Where were we?

98
00:06:48,608 --> 00:06:52,101
Well, what we were trying to figure out
if we want a jury trial or a bench trial.

99
00:06:52,279 --> 00:06:53,679
Yeah.

100
00:06:54,347 --> 00:06:55,781
Ben?

101
00:06:57,017 --> 00:06:58,246
Ben?

102
00:06:58,418 --> 00:06:59,442
I'll be right back.

103
00:07:41,227 --> 00:07:42,388
Hmm.

104
00:07:42,562 --> 00:07:44,963
Ben, what are you doing?

105
00:07:45,131 --> 00:07:46,690
Um...

106
00:07:47,334 --> 00:07:49,496
Come on, Ben, we
gotta get this done.

107
00:07:56,776 --> 00:07:58,540
- Craig.
- Hey, Cliff.

108
00:07:58,712 --> 00:08:00,704
Uh, do you have a
moment? I need to talk to you.

109
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:03,645
- You alone?
- Well, uh, Ben's upstairs.

110
00:08:03,817 --> 00:08:05,911
Oh. Then let's talk out here.

111
00:08:07,754 --> 00:08:09,188
All right.

112
00:08:10,523 --> 00:08:12,203
Hey, that's some great
press you're getting

113
00:08:12,359 --> 00:08:14,419
- on the Cameron Ivers case.
- Yeah.

114
00:08:14,594 --> 00:08:18,087
- Listen, uh, I've got a problem.
- What's up?

115
00:08:18,264 --> 00:08:20,495
I'm being blackmailed.

116
00:08:20,967 --> 00:08:24,267
You see, I met this
woman recently and, uh...

117
00:08:24,437 --> 00:08:28,966
Well, she and I have
gotten real close real fast.

118
00:08:29,442 --> 00:08:33,846
Oh, Craig, I thought
you were happily married.

119
00:08:34,180 --> 00:08:38,675
Well, I thought so too, but
then I met Rachel, and, uh...

120
00:08:40,153 --> 00:08:42,179
Cliff, it's like I never
had sex before.

121
00:08:42,355 --> 00:08:44,654
I mean, with her,
it's just incredible.

122
00:08:44,824 --> 00:08:46,864
The more I'm with her, the
more I wanna be with her.

123
00:08:47,027 --> 00:08:49,622
I mean, it's like I'm addicted.
You know what I mean?

124
00:08:49,796 --> 00:08:51,662
No, not really, but go ahead.

125
00:08:51,831 --> 00:08:54,426
Yeah, well, anyway, I
thought we were being discreet,

126
00:08:54,601 --> 00:08:57,935
but yesterday I got some rather
graphic photos of us along with a note

127
00:08:58,104 --> 00:09:00,039
saying the news media
would get copies next

128
00:09:00,206 --> 00:09:02,072
if I didn't do as I was told.

129
00:09:03,743 --> 00:09:07,942
Cliff, my wife is running
for a state assembly seat.

130
00:09:08,114 --> 00:09:09,776
This kind of publicity
could ruin her.

131
00:09:09,949 --> 00:09:12,851
I mean, I may not love her, but
I certainly don't wanna hurt her.

132
00:09:13,019 --> 00:09:14,282
She's a good person.

133
00:09:14,654 --> 00:09:16,987
Well, how much does
the blackmailer want?

134
00:09:17,157 --> 00:09:20,855
I don't know yet. I'm supposed to meet
with him this afternoon at Kelbo's Bar.

135
00:09:21,361 --> 00:09:23,057
I need a favor.

136
00:09:23,596 --> 00:09:26,122
No, no. On second thought,
Cliff, I'm gonna do this right.

137
00:09:26,299 --> 00:09:27,892
- I wanna hire you.
- What for?

138
00:09:28,068 --> 00:09:30,230
Go to Kelbo's in my place.

139
00:09:30,403 --> 00:09:32,003
You want me to meet
with the blackmailer?

140
00:09:32,172 --> 00:09:34,372
Well, I can't meet with him
in a public place like that.

141
00:09:34,507 --> 00:09:36,305
Everybody knows me.

142
00:09:37,277 --> 00:09:39,872
Jeez, Craig, I don't know. I
mean, it's really weird, you know?

143
00:09:40,046 --> 00:09:42,948
Cliff, I don't have
anyone else to ask.

144
00:09:43,116 --> 00:09:45,711
Now, this is a really
delicate situation.

145
00:09:45,885 --> 00:09:48,912
- I have to have someone I can trust.
- Yeah.

146
00:09:49,622 --> 00:09:52,421
But I don't want you to do it if it
makes you feel uncomfortable.

147
00:09:52,992 --> 00:09:54,893
I'll figure out something
else. Thanks, anyway.

148
00:09:55,061 --> 00:09:58,054
Well, hold, hold... Hold on.

149
00:09:59,199 --> 00:10:01,100
How am I supposed
to recognize the guy?

150
00:10:02,368 --> 00:10:05,361
He'll be sitting at a table by
himself with a pin in his lapel.

151
00:10:05,672 --> 00:10:07,664
The American flag.

152
00:10:08,241 --> 00:10:09,800
Thanks, Cliff.

153
00:10:25,558 --> 00:10:27,083
Can we get a pitcher?

154
00:10:27,727 --> 00:10:29,491
Yeah, yeah, great.

155
00:10:36,903 --> 00:10:38,263
Waiting for Craig
Browning, right?

156
00:10:38,404 --> 00:10:40,896
- Who the hell are you?
- Cliff Lewis.

157
00:10:41,074 --> 00:10:42,269
He sent me in his place.

158
00:10:42,442 --> 00:10:43,876
Aw, he shouldn't have done that.

159
00:10:44,043 --> 00:10:47,741
Wait... Oh, I'm sorry.
I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

160
00:10:47,914 --> 00:10:49,849
I'm sorry. Let me...

161
00:10:50,950 --> 00:10:53,283
I'm not a cop or anything.

162
00:10:53,620 --> 00:10:55,418
I'm Craig's lawyer.

163
00:10:55,588 --> 00:10:58,820
He just didn't wanna risk being
seen in public with you, that's all.

164
00:11:02,762 --> 00:11:04,321
So how much do you want?

165
00:11:04,497 --> 00:11:06,489
Oh, I don't want money.

166
00:11:06,766 --> 00:11:08,701
I want Cameron
Ivers to go to prison.

167
00:11:08,868 --> 00:11:11,997
Cam... Cameron,
his client? Ivers...

168
00:11:12,172 --> 00:11:13,196
That's right.

169
00:11:13,606 --> 00:11:15,905
I want Craig Browning
to throw the case.

170
00:11:17,043 --> 00:11:18,636
If Cameron Ivers goes to prison,

171
00:11:18,811 --> 00:11:20,939
then I tear up the
negatives of those pictures,

172
00:11:21,114 --> 00:11:23,049
and everybody's happy.

173
00:11:23,216 --> 00:11:24,775
Ivers walks,

174
00:11:24,951 --> 00:11:26,920
and I send those
pictures to the press

175
00:11:27,320 --> 00:11:29,448
and they have a field day.

176
00:11:29,856 --> 00:11:34,453
So all Browning's
gotta do to win is to lose.

177
00:11:35,261 --> 00:11:37,287
It's that simple.

178
00:11:37,931 --> 00:11:42,062
Tell him that, Mr. Lawyer.

179
00:13:46,492 --> 00:13:48,893
Taxi. Taxi!

180
00:14:47,153 --> 00:14:48,485
Yeah?

181
00:14:48,654 --> 00:14:51,453
Yeah, it's Cliff Lewis.
I wanna talk to you.

182
00:14:51,624 --> 00:14:52,819
Get lost.

183
00:14:54,360 --> 00:14:58,297
Cliff Lewis. I'm not leaving
here until I talk to you.

184
00:15:02,335 --> 00:15:03,803
Let me guess.

185
00:15:03,970 --> 00:15:05,836
You want your handkerchief back.

186
00:15:06,005 --> 00:15:07,598
No, I came here to make a deal.

187
00:15:07,774 --> 00:15:11,108
You got pictures, I got pictures. I
got pictures of you and two guys.

188
00:15:11,277 --> 00:15:14,145
Their names are John
Robinson and Mark Reeves.

189
00:15:14,313 --> 00:15:15,713
- Does that ring a bell?
- Who?

190
00:15:15,882 --> 00:15:17,682
"Who?" The two guys
who put you up to do this.

191
00:15:17,817 --> 00:15:19,417
You don't know what
you're talking about.

192
00:15:19,585 --> 00:15:21,816
You take Craig Browning
down, I'll take you all down.

193
00:15:21,988 --> 00:15:23,217
Is that understood, Mr. Aston?

194
00:15:23,389 --> 00:15:26,655
My, my, how clever you
are. You know my name.

195
00:15:26,826 --> 00:15:28,818
And where I'm staying.

196
00:15:28,995 --> 00:15:31,863
But I wonder how clever
Craig Browning will think you are

197
00:15:32,031 --> 00:15:34,728
when his picture appears
in all those papers.

198
00:15:34,901 --> 00:15:36,893
Now, get out.

199
00:15:53,853 --> 00:15:55,822
Housekeeping.

200
00:16:24,217 --> 00:16:25,913
- Hey, Ben.
- Yeah.

201
00:16:26,085 --> 00:16:29,920
Got some doughnuts.
Glazed or jelly filled.

202
00:16:30,089 --> 00:16:31,523
You eat them.

203
00:16:31,691 --> 00:16:34,422
- Hey, when's your reunion?
- Twenty-third.

204
00:16:34,594 --> 00:16:36,222
- Oh, getting excited?
- Can't wait.

205
00:16:36,395 --> 00:16:38,296
Oh, great.

206
00:16:39,332 --> 00:16:41,426
Glazed or jelly filled?

207
00:16:41,601 --> 00:16:43,866
- Is that all you're having?
- Yeah.

208
00:16:44,036 --> 00:16:46,335
What, are you on some
kind of diet or something?

209
00:16:46,506 --> 00:16:48,202
I look like I need
to be on a diet?

210
00:16:49,542 --> 00:16:53,104
No. No, no. I, uh... Just,
you know, was asking.

211
00:16:53,279 --> 00:16:54,804
I'll get that.

212
00:17:05,124 --> 00:17:06,615
- Norman.
- Hello, Cliff.

213
00:17:06,792 --> 00:17:08,488
Hi, hi.

214
00:17:10,530 --> 00:17:11,623
What can I do for you?

215
00:17:11,964 --> 00:17:13,398
I'm investigating the murder

216
00:17:13,566 --> 00:17:15,592
of a private investigator
from Savannah.

217
00:17:17,603 --> 00:17:19,299
Recognize him?

218
00:17:20,740 --> 00:17:22,299
I can't tell you that.

219
00:17:23,843 --> 00:17:26,642
- Why not?
- I can't tell you that either.

220
00:17:26,812 --> 00:17:28,075
Oh, hi, Norm.

221
00:17:29,215 --> 00:17:31,343
- Morning, Ben.
- What's going on?

222
00:17:32,018 --> 00:17:33,884
A maid at the hotel
where this man was staying

223
00:17:34,053 --> 00:17:37,114
saw somebody pounding on
the door to his room last night

224
00:17:37,290 --> 00:17:39,782
about an hour before
she found him dead.

225
00:17:39,959 --> 00:17:43,293
Heard the guy say his name
was Cliff or Clark or something.

226
00:17:43,462 --> 00:17:44,987
So when we found
Cliff's business card

227
00:17:45,164 --> 00:17:47,190
in one of the dead
man's pockets,

228
00:17:47,366 --> 00:17:49,961
we naturally thought
we should check it out.

229
00:17:50,136 --> 00:17:52,935
You fit the description
she gave of the guy to a T.

230
00:17:54,640 --> 00:17:55,869
I'm sorry.

231
00:17:56,042 --> 00:17:57,908
I can't say anything
more about any of this.

232
00:17:58,077 --> 00:18:00,672
I really am sorry, Norm.

233
00:18:02,949 --> 00:18:06,010
There will be a next time.

234
00:18:06,185 --> 00:18:07,585
Right.

235
00:18:08,054 --> 00:18:10,148
Sorry for the wasted trip.

236
00:18:11,591 --> 00:18:12,615
What's going on?

237
00:18:13,593 --> 00:18:16,062
Ben, I'm so sorry. I can't
tell you about this either.

238
00:18:18,564 --> 00:18:20,192
I gotta go.

239
00:18:20,366 --> 00:18:21,390
- Hi. Who was that?
- Hi.

240
00:18:21,567 --> 00:18:22,660
We got some work to do.

241
00:18:22,835 --> 00:18:24,235
Bye.

242
00:18:29,542 --> 00:18:30,771
What I'm trying to say

243
00:18:30,943 --> 00:18:33,674
is we're gonna have to
cool it for a little while.

244
00:18:35,314 --> 00:18:37,783
- You mean not see each other?
- Just for a couple of weeks.

245
00:18:37,950 --> 00:18:38,974
Why?

246
00:18:39,151 --> 00:18:41,677
A few complications
have arisen, that's all.

247
00:18:41,854 --> 00:18:43,516
What complications?
What happened?

248
00:18:43,689 --> 00:18:45,885
Rachel, believe
me, I wanna see you.

249
00:18:46,325 --> 00:18:49,352
As soon as things
calm down, I'll call you.

250
00:18:49,528 --> 00:18:51,190
I promise.

251
00:18:51,530 --> 00:18:53,658
Craig, I love you.

252
00:18:53,866 --> 00:18:56,131
I want us to be together.

253
00:19:00,539 --> 00:19:03,373
I can't, I gotta be in court.

254
00:19:06,879 --> 00:19:08,108
Three weeks tops.

255
00:19:09,615 --> 00:19:11,345
I swear.

256
00:19:16,422 --> 00:19:17,913
Am I okay?

257
00:19:25,564 --> 00:19:28,159
What's the lighting like on the
road where the accident occurred?

258
00:19:28,334 --> 00:19:29,597
There is no lighting.

259
00:19:29,769 --> 00:19:33,433
So if a person was driving
along that road at 11:15 at night,

260
00:19:33,606 --> 00:19:35,370
how easy would it
be to see something

261
00:19:35,541 --> 00:19:37,237
outside the beam of
his or her headlights?

262
00:19:37,410 --> 00:19:38,503
It wouldn't be easy,

263
00:19:38,678 --> 00:19:41,477
but it'd be even harder if
that person were speeding.

264
00:19:41,647 --> 00:19:43,928
Are you suggesting that my
client was speeding that night?

265
00:19:44,083 --> 00:19:46,211
Well, from the length
of the skid marks,

266
00:19:46,385 --> 00:19:47,853
the damage done to the car

267
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:50,216
and the injuries
sustained by the victim,

268
00:19:50,389 --> 00:19:52,654
we estimate he was
doing 50 miles an hour.

269
00:19:52,825 --> 00:19:55,226
- Posted speed limit's 35.
- You estimate?

270
00:19:55,928 --> 00:19:57,590
You mean, it's
possible Mr. Ivers

271
00:19:57,763 --> 00:20:00,562
- may had been going less than 50?
- Yeah, but it's also possible...

272
00:20:00,733 --> 00:20:02,895
Now, about those skid
marks you just mentioned,

273
00:20:03,069 --> 00:20:05,004
on what part of the
road did you find them?

274
00:20:05,171 --> 00:20:07,367
On a straightaway or a curve?

275
00:20:07,540 --> 00:20:10,704
Both. They began on a curve
and ended on a straightaway.

276
00:20:10,876 --> 00:20:12,936
Okay, so Mr. Ivers
came around the curve,

277
00:20:13,112 --> 00:20:15,809
saw the victim standing in the
road, and slammed on his brakes?

278
00:20:15,981 --> 00:20:17,506
- That's correct.
- All right, now,

279
00:20:17,683 --> 00:20:18,844
taking into consideration

280
00:20:19,018 --> 00:20:21,681
the length of the skid marks
and the point of impact,

281
00:20:21,854 --> 00:20:23,846
when did Mr. Ivers
start braking?

282
00:20:24,023 --> 00:20:27,960
As soon as he saw the victim or
an intoxicated moment or two later?

283
00:20:28,127 --> 00:20:30,756
Well, it's hard to say without
knowing how fast he was going.

284
00:20:30,930 --> 00:20:33,365
I thought you knew
how fast he was going.

285
00:20:33,532 --> 00:20:35,057
Oh, no, that's right.

286
00:20:35,234 --> 00:20:38,500
That was just an
estimate, wasn't it?

287
00:20:39,038 --> 00:20:40,973
Uh, one more thing, lieutenant.

288
00:20:41,140 --> 00:20:42,780
I understand you
found a set of footprints

289
00:20:42,908 --> 00:20:45,148
at the side of the road that
were not made by the victim.

290
00:20:45,311 --> 00:20:46,631
- Is that right?
- That's correct.

291
00:20:46,779 --> 00:20:48,145
Have you been able to determine

292
00:20:48,314 --> 00:20:50,579
- who did make those footprints?
- No.

293
00:20:50,750 --> 00:20:52,378
Yet one of them was
found directly on top

294
00:20:52,551 --> 00:20:54,452
of one of the victim's
footprints, wasn't it?

295
00:20:54,787 --> 00:20:58,121
- That's correct.
- Which indicates what?

296
00:20:58,791 --> 00:21:01,488
It's possible that someone
else was there that night.

297
00:21:02,328 --> 00:21:06,356
Well, it's possible that this
as yet unidentified person

298
00:21:06,532 --> 00:21:08,228
may have pushed
the victim onto the road

299
00:21:08,400 --> 00:21:10,665
- and caused the accident, isn't it?
- Objection.

300
00:21:10,836 --> 00:21:12,464
Calls for rampant speculation.

301
00:21:12,872 --> 00:21:14,238
Sustained.

302
00:21:15,107 --> 00:21:16,632
Nothing further, Your Honor.

303
00:21:17,476 --> 00:21:18,967
Next witness, Mr. Gerard.

304
00:21:24,383 --> 00:21:25,407
I'm sorry.

305
00:21:25,785 --> 00:21:27,879
Seeing Mr. Robinson
without an appointment

306
00:21:28,053 --> 00:21:29,453
is out of the question.

307
00:21:29,622 --> 00:21:34,253
Oh, but can't I just slip in after
he's done with, uh, Mr. Herman?

308
00:21:35,828 --> 00:21:37,956
No, you may not.

309
00:21:40,099 --> 00:21:41,965
Yes? Hello.

310
00:21:42,268 --> 00:21:46,131
Mm-hm. Mm... Mm-hm.

311
00:21:46,305 --> 00:21:49,139
Sure. I'll bring them right in.

312
00:22:12,097 --> 00:22:14,066
Thank you, Doris.

313
00:22:16,836 --> 00:22:18,464
Where the hell
did this come from?

314
00:22:18,637 --> 00:22:20,230
From me.

315
00:22:21,173 --> 00:22:22,402
I'm sorry, Mr. Robinson.

316
00:22:22,575 --> 00:22:24,771
She's been pestering
me to let her see you.

317
00:22:24,944 --> 00:22:27,906
I told her... I'll
call Security.

318
00:22:28,080 --> 00:22:30,606
No, no, no, that's all right.

319
00:22:30,983 --> 00:22:32,823
Roger, we're gonna
have to continue this later.

320
00:22:32,985 --> 00:22:35,045
Oh, that's no problem.

321
00:22:40,593 --> 00:22:41,822
You can go too, Doris.

322
00:22:46,899 --> 00:22:49,801
Hi. I'm Jerri Stone, I'm
a private investigator.

323
00:22:49,969 --> 00:22:53,462
I was just wondering
what you and Mr. Reeves

324
00:22:53,639 --> 00:22:56,632
were talking to Scott
Aston about in the park.

325
00:22:56,809 --> 00:22:58,929
I don't think that's any of
your business, Miss Stone.

326
00:22:59,445 --> 00:23:01,539
Well, it's just so obvious

327
00:23:01,714 --> 00:23:03,410
that you were meeting
with him on the sly,

328
00:23:03,582 --> 00:23:07,349
and since Mr. Aston
is so dead now...

329
00:23:09,021 --> 00:23:11,013
As you undoubtedly know,

330
00:23:11,190 --> 00:23:14,718
my partner and I are what is
known as corporate raiders.

331
00:23:15,227 --> 00:23:17,958
Sometimes we buy whole
companies. But before we do that,

332
00:23:18,130 --> 00:23:20,010
we check those companies
out from top to bottom.

333
00:23:20,165 --> 00:23:21,189
And to do that,

334
00:23:21,367 --> 00:23:24,428
we hire private
investigators like Scott Aston.

335
00:23:24,670 --> 00:23:27,697
And if you ask me what company
he was checking out, I won't tell you.

336
00:23:27,873 --> 00:23:29,171
You know, that's so interesting

337
00:23:29,341 --> 00:23:31,537
because that's not at all
what Mr. Reeves told me

338
00:23:31,710 --> 00:23:33,201
when I showed him the picture.

339
00:23:34,079 --> 00:23:35,877
When did you talk to him?

340
00:23:36,048 --> 00:23:39,018
This morning. About an hour ago.

341
00:23:39,952 --> 00:23:41,545
It's a nice try, Miss Stone.

342
00:23:41,720 --> 00:23:42,847
Mark's in L.A. on business.

343
00:23:43,622 --> 00:23:46,615
I just talked to him
about an hour ago.

344
00:23:48,694 --> 00:23:50,492
Why did you take
this picture anyway?

345
00:23:52,031 --> 00:23:53,260
Why, Mr. Robinson,

346
00:23:53,432 --> 00:23:55,162
I don't think that's
any of your business,

347
00:23:55,334 --> 00:23:56,802
but thanks for your time.

348
00:24:06,946 --> 00:24:08,744
- Hey.
- Hey.

349
00:24:08,914 --> 00:24:10,644
- Hey, I gotta talk to you.
- Yeah.

350
00:24:10,816 --> 00:24:13,411
Uh, did you hear about this
private investigator, Scott Aston,

351
00:24:13,585 --> 00:24:15,866
- who got murdered last night?
- Yeah, it was on the news.

352
00:24:16,021 --> 00:24:18,650
Well, that's the guy who
was blackmailing you.

353
00:24:18,824 --> 00:24:21,293
- You're kidding.
- No, and it gets worse than that.

354
00:24:21,460 --> 00:24:22,792
I went to see him last night.

355
00:24:22,962 --> 00:24:26,763
And the police found out, so
I'm kind of on their suspect list.

356
00:24:26,932 --> 00:24:28,059
You went to see him?

357
00:24:28,233 --> 00:24:30,464
Yeah, well, I had him
followed from Kelbo's.

358
00:24:30,636 --> 00:24:32,161
And, you know, once I found out

359
00:24:32,338 --> 00:24:34,307
who he was and
where he was staying,

360
00:24:34,473 --> 00:24:36,032
I confronted him.

361
00:24:36,208 --> 00:24:37,648
You did all this
without telling me?

362
00:24:37,810 --> 00:24:40,290
Jeez, Cliff, I hired you to meet
with the guy, and that was it.

363
00:24:40,446 --> 00:24:42,779
Well, I'm going the extra
mile. Now, don't worry.

364
00:24:42,948 --> 00:24:45,747
I didn't say anything to
the police, I don't intend to.

365
00:24:45,918 --> 00:24:49,377
It falls under the
lawyer-client privilege.

366
00:24:49,855 --> 00:24:52,290
All right. Keep me
posted, in private.

367
00:24:52,458 --> 00:24:53,756
Yeah, well, that's a problem.

368
00:24:53,926 --> 00:24:56,327
I don't know where
it's safe to contact you.

369
00:24:56,495 --> 00:24:59,795
- Try me at the office.
- What if you're not at the office?

370
00:24:59,965 --> 00:25:03,299
Try me at the Mayfield Hotel.
Mr. and Mrs. William Gillespie.

371
00:25:03,469 --> 00:25:05,131
- William Gillespie?
- Yeah.

372
00:25:05,304 --> 00:25:06,795
Mayfield Hotel. Right.

373
00:25:07,573 --> 00:25:09,667
- Hey. Just the person I wanna see.
- Hey.

374
00:25:09,842 --> 00:25:12,038
Jerri Stone, Craig
Browning. Craig, Jerri.

375
00:25:12,211 --> 00:25:13,235
- Hi, Craig.
- Hi.

376
00:25:13,412 --> 00:25:15,252
Did you have a chance
to talk to John Robinson?

377
00:25:15,414 --> 00:25:16,882
Yep.

378
00:25:18,117 --> 00:25:20,018
They paid Scott
Aston to spy for them?

379
00:25:20,185 --> 00:25:23,587
- That's what he said.
- I don't buy that for a moment.

380
00:25:28,927 --> 00:25:31,556
Rachel, what are you doing here?

381
00:25:33,265 --> 00:25:37,225
It's okay. Cliff Lewis, Jerri Stone,
this is my friend Rachel Bauer.

382
00:25:38,070 --> 00:25:39,436
- Hi.
- Hi.

383
00:25:39,605 --> 00:25:42,666
- Hi.
- This will just take a minute.

384
00:25:43,242 --> 00:25:44,266
What're you doing?

385
00:25:44,810 --> 00:25:46,870
I'm sorry. I know I
wasn't supposed to come,

386
00:25:47,046 --> 00:25:48,241
but I don't like this.

387
00:25:48,414 --> 00:25:49,414
I need to talk to you.

388
00:25:49,581 --> 00:25:51,413
Not now. I'll call
you, I promise.

389
00:25:51,583 --> 00:25:52,744
I miss you.

390
00:25:52,918 --> 00:25:55,319
I'll speak to you later, okay?

391
00:26:00,793 --> 00:26:02,591
So that was her, huh?

392
00:26:04,296 --> 00:26:07,289
Yeah. Remember, keep me posted.

393
00:26:15,274 --> 00:26:16,708
What?

394
00:26:16,942 --> 00:26:18,342
His car.

395
00:26:18,510 --> 00:26:20,950
It was outside Kelbo's when
you went to meet with Scott Aston.

396
00:26:21,080 --> 00:26:23,345
It was parked right next to me.

397
00:26:39,932 --> 00:26:41,867
Who was at the door?

398
00:26:42,334 --> 00:26:43,358
Lieutenant Bigelow.

399
00:26:45,003 --> 00:26:47,632
- He's in the living room.
- What for?

400
00:26:47,806 --> 00:26:50,970
Well, a handkerchief
with your initials on it

401
00:26:51,143 --> 00:26:56,707
and Scott Aston's blood was
found at the scene of the crime.

402
00:26:57,516 --> 00:26:59,314
He wants to take you downtown.

403
00:27:21,140 --> 00:27:22,836
Mr. Matlock.

404
00:27:23,208 --> 00:27:25,677
Hi. Craig Browning.

405
00:27:26,245 --> 00:27:27,474
I know who you are.

406
00:27:27,646 --> 00:27:30,275
- I just heard that Cliff's been arrested.
- Suspicion of murder.

407
00:27:30,449 --> 00:27:32,509
Oh, my God.

408
00:27:32,818 --> 00:27:35,447
- Are you gonna represent him?
- Yeah.

409
00:27:35,621 --> 00:27:36,748
And as his lawyer,

410
00:27:36,922 --> 00:27:40,290
I have to keep whatever
he tells me a secret.

411
00:27:40,526 --> 00:27:42,358
He told me everything.

412
00:27:43,428 --> 00:27:45,021
You mean about me?

413
00:27:45,197 --> 00:27:47,189
And now you're gonna
tell me everything,

414
00:27:47,366 --> 00:27:51,064
starting with why you were at
Kelbo's the afternoon you sent Cliff

415
00:27:51,236 --> 00:27:54,434
- to see that blackmailer.
- What makes you think I was there?

416
00:27:54,606 --> 00:27:56,632
And whatever you do, don't lie.

417
00:27:56,808 --> 00:27:58,640
I hate that.

418
00:28:00,579 --> 00:28:02,899
I just wanted to get a look
at the blackmailer, that's all.

419
00:28:03,048 --> 00:28:04,812
- Why?
- I was curious.

420
00:28:04,983 --> 00:28:08,317
You sure you didn't follow
him back to his hotel room?

421
00:28:08,487 --> 00:28:10,718
What are you trying to
say, that I killed Scott Aston?

422
00:28:10,889 --> 00:28:12,323
Cliff didn't.

423
00:28:12,491 --> 00:28:16,553
The only one I know who
benefits from Scott Aston's murder

424
00:28:16,728 --> 00:28:19,789
and from this
lawyer-client relationship

425
00:28:19,965 --> 00:28:21,695
that prevents Cliff
from telling the police

426
00:28:21,867 --> 00:28:23,358
anything he knows is you.

427
00:28:27,739 --> 00:28:30,004
You think I set
this whole thing up?

428
00:28:30,175 --> 00:28:33,236
It wouldn't be the first time
somebody's taken advantage of Cliff.

429
00:28:33,412 --> 00:28:35,210
Well, you're wrong.

430
00:28:35,547 --> 00:28:36,947
Then prove it.

431
00:28:37,115 --> 00:28:40,381
Go in there and release him
from any and all obligations to you.

432
00:28:40,552 --> 00:28:42,453
Let him tell the truth.

433
00:28:44,489 --> 00:28:46,253
The person who hired Scott Aston

434
00:28:46,425 --> 00:28:48,553
to make sure I threw
Cameron Ivers' case,

435
00:28:48,727 --> 00:28:51,026
that's who you should
be trying to nail, not me.

436
00:28:51,196 --> 00:28:53,097
I'm no murderer.

437
00:28:53,565 --> 00:28:54,897
A coward, maybe,

438
00:28:55,467 --> 00:28:57,368
but not a murderer.

439
00:29:15,387 --> 00:29:16,912
Excuse me.

440
00:29:17,089 --> 00:29:20,025
Uh, this man, Scott Aston,
came in here two days ago

441
00:29:20,192 --> 00:29:21,387
around 4:00 in the afternoon.

442
00:29:21,560 --> 00:29:22,960
Do either of you remember him?

443
00:29:23,128 --> 00:29:25,290
Yeah, as a matter of fact, I do.

444
00:29:25,464 --> 00:29:28,400
I'm investigating his murder. Do you
happen to remember what he wanted?

445
00:29:28,567 --> 00:29:29,847
Well, that's why I remember him.

446
00:29:30,002 --> 00:29:33,166
He came in here claiming that
we'd run some obituaries last month

447
00:29:33,338 --> 00:29:35,239
on some people who
weren't really dead.

448
00:29:35,407 --> 00:29:38,707
Made us give him copies
of their death certificates.

449
00:29:38,877 --> 00:29:40,677
You keep copies of
death certificates in here?

450
00:29:40,812 --> 00:29:44,613
Well, Legal Department won't
let us publish an obit without one.

451
00:29:45,117 --> 00:29:47,643
Do you happen to remember
which ones you showed him?

452
00:29:47,819 --> 00:29:51,779
No, but the computer will.

453
00:29:58,730 --> 00:30:01,825
Having previously found the
defendant guilty of the lesser charge

454
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:03,662
of vehicular homicide,

455
00:30:03,835 --> 00:30:07,033
I sentence him to five
years formal probation,

456
00:30:07,205 --> 00:30:09,572
order him to pay a $10,000 fine,

457
00:30:09,741 --> 00:30:13,735
and to complete 1,000
hours of community service.

458
00:30:13,912 --> 00:30:16,404
This court is now in recess.

459
00:30:19,918 --> 00:30:22,888
- How can I not?
- I think so too.

460
00:30:25,490 --> 00:30:27,288
Good job.

461
00:30:28,226 --> 00:30:29,717
- Uh, Mr. Ivers.
- Yeah.

462
00:30:29,895 --> 00:30:31,095
- Ben Matlock.
- How do you do?

463
00:30:31,263 --> 00:30:32,663
Could I speak with
you for a moment?

464
00:30:32,798 --> 00:30:35,495
- I suppose.
- Okay, come on in here.

465
00:30:38,370 --> 00:30:40,965
This may seem like
a strange question,

466
00:30:41,139 --> 00:30:44,803
but, uh, who would benefit
from your going to prison?

467
00:30:45,510 --> 00:30:46,534
What is this?

468
00:30:46,712 --> 00:30:48,392
Craig asked me the
same thing two days ago.

469
00:30:48,547 --> 00:30:51,176
- What'd you tell him?
- That I had no idea.

470
00:30:51,350 --> 00:30:53,683
Now, what's this about?
Craig wouldn't say.

471
00:30:53,852 --> 00:30:56,822
Somebody tried to blackmail
him into throwing your case

472
00:30:56,988 --> 00:30:58,388
so you'd wind up in prison.

473
00:30:58,557 --> 00:31:00,617
- Are you joking?
- No.

474
00:31:00,792 --> 00:31:04,354
It was this man here. Private
investigator Scott Aston.

475
00:31:04,529 --> 00:31:06,157
- You know him?
- No.

476
00:31:06,331 --> 00:31:08,562
But I sure as hell
know these other two.

477
00:31:08,734 --> 00:31:10,032
But, Mr. Ivers...?

478
00:31:10,202 --> 00:31:12,034
Mr. Ivers, stop.
You can't go in there.

479
00:31:12,938 --> 00:31:14,065
What's the matter?

480
00:31:14,239 --> 00:31:15,959
Were you afraid I was
gonna buy some company

481
00:31:16,108 --> 00:31:17,668
out from under your
snotty little noses?

482
00:31:17,809 --> 00:31:19,289
That why you wanted
me out of the way?

483
00:31:19,444 --> 00:31:21,970
- Take it easy. What's the problem?
- Right here.

484
00:31:22,147 --> 00:31:23,809
This is the problem.

485
00:31:23,982 --> 00:31:25,245
That's you.

486
00:31:25,417 --> 00:31:28,216
And that's you, meeting with
the guy who tried to get my lawyer

487
00:31:28,387 --> 00:31:29,987
- to send me up the river.
- I don't know

488
00:31:30,155 --> 00:31:31,835
- what you're talking about.
- He was doing

489
00:31:31,990 --> 00:31:34,152
- some investigating for us, that's all.
- Liar.

490
00:31:34,326 --> 00:31:36,295
- You're both liars!
- Mr. Ivers, please.

491
00:31:36,461 --> 00:31:38,191
Calm down, Cameron.

492
00:31:38,363 --> 00:31:40,298
That meeting had
nothing to do with you.

493
00:31:40,465 --> 00:31:41,558
I think it did.

494
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:43,500
In fact, I know it did.

495
00:31:45,437 --> 00:31:49,340
You two still have a 28 percent
stake in Lavinia Enterprises?

496
00:31:52,477 --> 00:31:53,706
Good.

497
00:31:53,879 --> 00:31:57,338
Because I just got through
dumping 250,000 shares of it.

498
00:32:00,018 --> 00:32:04,114
Don't look now, boys,
but you're bleeding.

499
00:32:17,068 --> 00:32:20,232
Thanks for paying my bail,
Pop. I'll pay you back, I swear.

500
00:32:20,405 --> 00:32:22,931
I don't know, Cliff.
Two hundred grand.

501
00:32:23,108 --> 00:32:24,468
You may have to
get a paper route.

502
00:32:26,044 --> 00:32:28,809
Besides, I'm gonna get it back.

503
00:32:28,980 --> 00:32:31,950
Unless you're thinking about making
a run for the border or something.

504
00:32:32,117 --> 00:32:34,717
Tell you what I'm thinking about.
I'm thinking it was stupid of me

505
00:32:34,853 --> 00:32:36,493
to let Craig Browning
set me up like that.

506
00:32:36,655 --> 00:32:39,887
Now, you don't know for
sure that he set you up.

507
00:32:40,058 --> 00:32:42,459
You trusted him,
that's not stupid.

508
00:32:42,627 --> 00:32:45,256
Having faith in your fellow
man, that's not stupid.

509
00:32:45,430 --> 00:32:47,262
That's an admirable quality.

510
00:32:47,432 --> 00:32:49,560
Oh, Pop, I was trying to
impress him, you know?

511
00:32:49,734 --> 00:32:51,094
I just went overboard
on this case

512
00:32:51,236 --> 00:32:54,695
because I wanted him to
respect me and like me and...

513
00:32:55,173 --> 00:32:57,768
I wanted him to
think I was cool.

514
00:32:58,810 --> 00:33:01,075
Yeah, that's stupid.

515
00:33:14,793 --> 00:33:16,728
I'll get it.

516
00:33:25,504 --> 00:33:28,599
Ben, there's somebody
here to see you.

517
00:33:32,978 --> 00:33:35,470
- Oh, hi.
- Hello, again, Mr. Matlock.

518
00:33:35,647 --> 00:33:37,843
I'm sorry I took off like
that. I'm a bit impulsive.

519
00:33:38,016 --> 00:33:39,314
Where'd you go?

520
00:33:39,484 --> 00:33:40,679
Oh, hey, everyone.

521
00:33:40,852 --> 00:33:42,115
- Mr. Ivers.
- Hello.

522
00:33:42,287 --> 00:33:44,756
I went to see John
Robinson and Mark Reeves.

523
00:33:44,923 --> 00:33:46,603
They're up to something,
no doubt about it.

524
00:33:46,758 --> 00:33:48,038
But while I was in their office,

525
00:33:48,193 --> 00:33:49,673
- something very odd happened.
- What?

526
00:33:49,828 --> 00:33:51,262
Well, there was a
young woman there.

527
00:33:51,429 --> 00:33:54,524
I'd never seen her before,
and yet she knew who I was,

528
00:33:54,699 --> 00:33:58,136
which is very surprising.
I keep a very low profile.

529
00:33:58,470 --> 00:34:00,132
- Maybe she works for them.
- Mm-mm.

530
00:34:00,305 --> 00:34:02,137
I've been to their
offices dozens of times.

531
00:34:02,307 --> 00:34:03,947
I've been to parties
at both their houses.

532
00:34:04,075 --> 00:34:05,668
I've never seen
her before. I'm sure.

533
00:34:06,411 --> 00:34:07,470
What did she look like?

534
00:34:07,646 --> 00:34:10,377
Mid to late 20s, 5'6, 5'7,

535
00:34:10,549 --> 00:34:12,069
dark brown hair,
about shoulder length,

536
00:34:12,217 --> 00:34:14,049
trim, very pretty.

537
00:34:14,352 --> 00:34:16,912
- Fair skin?
- Yeah.

538
00:34:17,088 --> 00:34:19,023
Rachel Bauer.

539
00:34:21,893 --> 00:34:22,917
I promise you,

540
00:34:23,094 --> 00:34:24,934
as soon as the election
is over, I'll tell her.

541
00:34:25,096 --> 00:34:27,759
Even if you did, you
wouldn't wind up with me.

542
00:34:27,933 --> 00:34:31,165
I can't handle this anymore,
Craig. It hurts too much.

543
00:34:34,639 --> 00:34:36,972
Craig, it's Cliff. Open up.

544
00:34:38,276 --> 00:34:39,539
- Hey.
- Hey.

545
00:34:39,711 --> 00:34:42,476
I'm really sorry to
barge in on you like this.

546
00:34:42,647 --> 00:34:45,481
Hi, Rachel. I see you're
still on the job here.

547
00:34:45,650 --> 00:34:46,674
I guess no one told you.

548
00:34:46,851 --> 00:34:50,083
The blackmailer's dead and
Cameron Ivers was acquitted,

549
00:34:50,255 --> 00:34:52,383
so you can go home.

550
00:34:52,557 --> 00:34:53,820
What are you talking about?

551
00:34:53,992 --> 00:34:56,723
Well, Rachel here
was seen this morning

552
00:34:56,895 --> 00:34:59,592
at the offices of John
Robinson and Mark Reeves.

553
00:34:59,764 --> 00:35:04,065
I'm pretty sure those are the guys
who hired Aston to blackmail you.

554
00:35:04,536 --> 00:35:05,696
Which, if you think about it,

555
00:35:05,837 --> 00:35:07,635
he couldn't have
done without her help.

556
00:35:07,806 --> 00:35:10,605
So that's why you're
blowing me off?

557
00:35:11,910 --> 00:35:13,708
All along, you were
working with Scott Aston,

558
00:35:14,479 --> 00:35:16,971
leading me on so he
could take those pictures.

559
00:35:18,049 --> 00:35:19,312
I don't have to listen to this.

560
00:35:20,151 --> 00:35:21,175
Rachel, listen.

561
00:35:21,353 --> 00:35:23,254
Here is something
you do have to listen to.

562
00:35:23,421 --> 00:35:25,287
Chances are that one
or both of those men

563
00:35:25,457 --> 00:35:27,688
you met with this
morning killed Scott Aston

564
00:35:27,859 --> 00:35:30,727
because I found a connection
between them and him.

565
00:35:30,895 --> 00:35:33,922
And now I've found a
connection between them and you.

566
00:35:34,265 --> 00:35:35,825
So, what do you
suppose they're gonna do

567
00:35:35,967 --> 00:35:37,765
when they figure that out?

568
00:35:38,069 --> 00:35:39,298
Rachel, go to the DA

569
00:35:39,471 --> 00:35:41,838
and get these guys
before they get you.

570
00:35:42,407 --> 00:35:44,342
There is nothing to tell the DA.

571
00:35:44,509 --> 00:35:48,640
John Robinson and Mark Reeves
paid me to seduce Craig, that's it.

572
00:35:48,813 --> 00:35:50,613
I don't know why they
wanted to blackmail him,

573
00:35:50,782 --> 00:35:52,148
and I know nothing
of the murders.

574
00:35:52,317 --> 00:35:53,877
Well, how long did
you know Scott Aston?

575
00:35:54,019 --> 00:35:56,420
I talked to him once, the
day before he was murdered.

576
00:35:56,588 --> 00:35:58,580
He was in a big hurry
to get down to Florida.

577
00:35:58,757 --> 00:36:00,487
You and me,

578
00:36:00,792 --> 00:36:02,317
the whole thing was an act?

579
00:36:03,762 --> 00:36:07,324
You used me for
fun away from home.

580
00:36:07,632 --> 00:36:10,192
I used you in a different way.

581
00:36:12,504 --> 00:36:14,803
Oh, if it's any consolation,

582
00:36:15,740 --> 00:36:18,266
I intend to vote for your wife.

583
00:36:31,022 --> 00:36:33,184
I'm not sure I follow you.

584
00:36:33,358 --> 00:36:36,760
Somebody wanted
Cameron Ivers to go to prison?

585
00:36:36,928 --> 00:36:38,089
Is that what you're saying?

586
00:36:38,263 --> 00:36:42,325
And I can't help but believe it
had something to do with business.

587
00:36:42,500 --> 00:36:46,528
Like, say, maybe he wanted
to buy somebody's company,

588
00:36:46,705 --> 00:36:49,937
say, yours, only somebody
else wanted him out of the way

589
00:36:50,108 --> 00:36:51,371
so they could buy your company.

590
00:36:51,543 --> 00:36:53,910
Say, John Robinson
and Mark Reeves.

591
00:36:54,079 --> 00:36:57,777
- I just met with John the other day.
- I know.

592
00:36:57,949 --> 00:37:00,248
He planning on buying you out?

593
00:37:01,419 --> 00:37:04,685
Mr. Matlock, answering that
question could land me in prison.

594
00:37:04,856 --> 00:37:07,985
You're asking for what
the SEC refers to as...

595
00:37:08,159 --> 00:37:09,684
That's insider information.

596
00:37:09,861 --> 00:37:13,354
I know, and I'm not looking to
make a fortune in the stock market.

597
00:37:13,531 --> 00:37:15,466
The police think my
associate's a murderer

598
00:37:15,633 --> 00:37:18,034
and I'm just trying
to prove he's not.

599
00:37:21,740 --> 00:37:24,767
What I'm about to say is
completely off the record.

600
00:37:24,943 --> 00:37:27,674
If anybody asks, I'll deny
that I ever said a thing.

601
00:37:27,846 --> 00:37:28,870
Of course.

602
00:37:29,047 --> 00:37:31,141
The only person of late
who has shown any interest

603
00:37:31,316 --> 00:37:34,878
in acquiring Ashfield
Life is Cameron Ivers.

604
00:37:35,053 --> 00:37:37,284
I was talking to John about
something else entirely.

605
00:37:37,455 --> 00:37:39,424
Have John Robinson
and Mark Reeves

606
00:37:39,924 --> 00:37:42,951
ever expressed an interest
in buying your company?

607
00:37:43,128 --> 00:37:44,152
Never. Never.

608
00:37:44,329 --> 00:37:45,888
Although I don't know why not.

609
00:37:46,064 --> 00:37:50,160
Our sales for the past two
years have been monumental.

610
00:37:50,335 --> 00:37:51,803
Oh, long as we're
on the subject,

611
00:37:51,970 --> 00:37:54,872
have you heard about the
flexible, premium adjustable policy

612
00:37:55,039 --> 00:37:57,099
that we've just begun offering?

613
00:39:52,690 --> 00:39:54,318
Ugh!

614
00:39:55,260 --> 00:39:57,092
Hello?

615
00:40:29,961 --> 00:40:31,896
Right, left, right,
left, walk it out.

616
00:40:32,063 --> 00:40:34,464
Right, left, watch my
feet. Watch my feet.

617
00:40:34,632 --> 00:40:36,112
Now, we're gonna
get into the Monster.

618
00:40:36,267 --> 00:40:38,896
Okay, here we go. Here we go.

619
00:40:39,070 --> 00:40:41,096
That's it. Now we
get the arms into it.

620
00:40:41,272 --> 00:40:43,104
- Ready?
- This is like when I tried to learn

621
00:40:43,274 --> 00:40:44,298
to do the foxtrot.

622
00:40:44,475 --> 00:40:45,499
Yeah, what happened?

623
00:40:45,677 --> 00:40:47,942
- I broke this woman's foot.
- Oh, yeah?

624
00:40:48,112 --> 00:40:49,432
- Let's do something else.
- Okay.

625
00:40:49,580 --> 00:40:50,860
- That's enough aerobics.
- Okay.

626
00:40:51,015 --> 00:40:52,244
- Good job.
- Yeah.

627
00:40:53,751 --> 00:40:56,915
Okay, we're gonna move
into some resistance training.

628
00:40:57,088 --> 00:40:58,852
Resistance.

629
00:40:59,958 --> 00:41:02,393
- There you go.
- Okay. Ugh.

630
00:41:02,794 --> 00:41:04,820
I don't wanna look like
Arnold Schwarzenegger.

631
00:41:04,996 --> 00:41:07,522
I just wanna lose 20
pounds by the 23rd.

632
00:41:07,699 --> 00:41:10,066
Let me tell you something.
If you wanna lose weight,

633
00:41:10,234 --> 00:41:14,638
the proper way to do it is to eat
right and increase your metabolism.

634
00:41:14,806 --> 00:41:15,830
- You got it?
- Yeah.

635
00:41:16,007 --> 00:41:17,475
Okay, you stick with me, Mr. M.,

636
00:41:17,642 --> 00:41:19,201
and you're gonna
be looking and feeling

637
00:41:19,377 --> 00:41:21,073
like a million bucks in no time.

638
00:41:21,245 --> 00:41:23,339
Sometimes I feel
that's what I'm paying.

639
00:41:25,249 --> 00:41:26,809
- You're a pretty funny guy.
- Ha-ha-ha.

640
00:41:26,951 --> 00:41:30,615
Okay, one-arm dumbbell
curls. Just like this:

641
00:41:30,788 --> 00:41:32,586
Shoulders back, chest out,

642
00:41:33,024 --> 00:41:34,083
right arm first.

643
00:41:34,258 --> 00:41:35,282
Bring it up, go ahead.

644
00:41:35,460 --> 00:41:36,689
Bring it up, you exhale.

645
00:41:36,861 --> 00:41:39,296
- Go ahead, right arm.
- Huff.

646
00:41:39,464 --> 00:41:41,023
That's it, get it up.

647
00:41:41,199 --> 00:41:42,667
That's it, keep going.

648
00:41:42,834 --> 00:41:44,359
That's it. That's
the way to breathe.

649
00:41:44,535 --> 00:41:47,004
Okay, and down slowly.

650
00:41:47,171 --> 00:41:48,662
There you go. Left arm up.

651
00:41:48,840 --> 00:41:50,706
- Come on, keep going.
- These are heavy.

652
00:41:50,875 --> 00:41:53,208
You're darned right they're
heavy. No pain, no gain.

653
00:41:53,378 --> 00:41:54,641
Come on now, squeeze.

654
00:41:54,812 --> 00:41:56,303
Keep it going, keep it going.

655
00:41:56,481 --> 00:41:59,645
Pretty soon your arms are
gonna feel like hot, molten lead.

656
00:41:59,817 --> 00:42:01,547
Yeah, they do.

657
00:42:01,719 --> 00:42:02,982
Ben?

658
00:42:03,154 --> 00:42:05,680
Oh, here, she might
wanna talk to me.

659
00:42:09,694 --> 00:42:11,322
Shh.

660
00:42:17,802 --> 00:42:19,634
What have you been doing?

661
00:42:19,804 --> 00:42:23,571
- Uh, moving furniture.
- Why?

662
00:42:23,741 --> 00:42:25,539
You could've asked
me to help you.

663
00:42:25,710 --> 00:42:28,646
I just felt like doing it.

664
00:42:28,813 --> 00:42:30,213
Oh.

665
00:42:30,381 --> 00:42:31,781
Well, I went over
to Cain Mortuary,

666
00:42:31,949 --> 00:42:34,069
you know, the place that
issued the death certificates

667
00:42:34,218 --> 00:42:36,658
to the three dead people that
Scott Aston was so interested in

668
00:42:36,788 --> 00:42:38,757
- at <i>The Atlanta Herald?</i>
- Yeah.

669
00:42:38,923 --> 00:42:41,051
Well, the place was
completely abandoned.

670
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:42,557
I mean, really eerie.

671
00:42:42,727 --> 00:42:46,323
Like nobody'd been there
for months, maybe even years.

672
00:42:46,564 --> 00:42:49,932
Well, they could've gone
out of business. Unh.

673
00:42:50,101 --> 00:42:53,003
Not according to the
Better Business Bureau.

674
00:42:54,572 --> 00:42:56,438
Are you okay?

675
00:42:56,741 --> 00:42:58,334
Oh, yeah, yeah. I'm fine.

676
00:42:58,509 --> 00:43:00,269
Well, they're still listed
in the phone book,

677
00:43:00,411 --> 00:43:02,380
and when you call,
you get a machine.

678
00:43:02,547 --> 00:43:03,776
But get this one,

679
00:43:03,948 --> 00:43:07,248
those people whose names
were on the death certificates?

680
00:43:07,418 --> 00:43:08,886
They're not dead.

681
00:43:09,821 --> 00:43:10,845
They're not?

682
00:43:11,956 --> 00:43:14,949
I traced them down through
their Social Security numbers.

683
00:43:15,126 --> 00:43:16,890
They're all very much alive.

684
00:43:17,061 --> 00:43:19,587
Which is what
Scott Aston thought.

685
00:43:21,032 --> 00:43:22,796
I wonder what he was on to.

686
00:43:22,967 --> 00:43:26,802
I don't know, but
maybe we better find out.

687
00:44:14,952 --> 00:44:17,217
Maybe a little onion.

688
00:44:46,484 --> 00:44:49,977
- Ben, you here?
- In the kitchen.

689
00:44:50,488 --> 00:44:53,390
- This place stinks like onions.
- Onions stink.

690
00:44:53,558 --> 00:44:54,890
They make you cry too.

691
00:44:55,059 --> 00:44:57,153
Did you talk to the DA yet?

692
00:44:57,662 --> 00:44:59,756
- About what?
- Cameron Ivers.

693
00:44:59,931 --> 00:45:02,400
- What about him?
- Someone hit him with a car last night

694
00:45:02,567 --> 00:45:04,399
- and killed him.
- I know that.

695
00:45:04,569 --> 00:45:07,971
Well, that proves that
Cliff didn't kill Scott Aston.

696
00:45:08,139 --> 00:45:10,734
- I don't get it.
- Use your noggin, Ben.

697
00:45:10,908 --> 00:45:14,367
Somebody hires Scott Aston to
blackmail Cameron Ivers' lawyer,

698
00:45:14,545 --> 00:45:17,606
so this fellow Ivers would
go to jail for hitting that guy

699
00:45:17,782 --> 00:45:19,011
when he was driving drunk.

700
00:45:19,283 --> 00:45:21,115
You told him Craig Browning

701
00:45:21,285 --> 00:45:23,083
- was being blackmailed?
- Ben, I didn't...

702
00:45:23,254 --> 00:45:25,052
What happened to
privileged information?

703
00:45:25,223 --> 00:45:26,863
You might as well
put it in the newspaper.

704
00:45:26,991 --> 00:45:30,291
- I didn't tell him.
- So when this fella Ivers went free,

705
00:45:30,461 --> 00:45:33,590
this somebody killed him
just like they killed Scott Aston.

706
00:45:33,764 --> 00:45:37,895
It's probably John Robinson
or, uh, Mark Reeves.

707
00:45:38,069 --> 00:45:39,093
Maybe even both.

708
00:45:39,270 --> 00:45:41,671
- Where'd you get all this stuff?
- I didn't tell him.

709
00:45:41,839 --> 00:45:45,173
- I heard you two talking the other day.
- You were eavesdropping.

710
00:45:45,343 --> 00:45:46,743
I just heard you.

711
00:45:46,911 --> 00:45:49,380
The only place Cliff and
I talked about this stuff

712
00:45:49,547 --> 00:45:51,379
was right here in the
kitchen. Where were you?

713
00:45:51,549 --> 00:45:52,915
Right there in that dining room.

714
00:45:53,217 --> 00:45:55,948
You were hiding in the
dining room listening to us.

715
00:45:56,120 --> 00:45:57,349
Daggone you, Billy.

716
00:45:57,521 --> 00:45:59,422
Pop, Ben is my lawyer.

717
00:45:59,590 --> 00:46:01,821
Any conversations we
have are confidential.

718
00:46:01,993 --> 00:46:04,326
Can I help it if I
got all my faculties?

719
00:46:04,495 --> 00:46:06,487
Doctor said I got
ears like a fruit bat.

720
00:46:06,664 --> 00:46:08,428
- Like a what?
- Fruit bat.

721
00:46:08,599 --> 00:46:10,727
Now, listen, Ben, Cliff
was with me last night.

722
00:46:10,901 --> 00:46:12,529
We had dinner, pot
roast and dumplings.

723
00:46:12,703 --> 00:46:14,638
Then we played gin
rummy till 10, and I won.

724
00:46:14,805 --> 00:46:16,405
So he couldn't have
killed Cameron Ivers,

725
00:46:16,574 --> 00:46:19,271
and that means he
didn't kill Scott Aston.

726
00:46:19,443 --> 00:46:23,175
Billy, I can't say anything to
the DA till Cliff says it's okay.

727
00:46:23,981 --> 00:46:26,348
- Tell him.
- I can't.

728
00:46:26,517 --> 00:46:28,383
They think you're
a murderer, tell him.

729
00:46:28,552 --> 00:46:31,488
It is the principle of the
thing, Dad. I can't tell him.

730
00:46:31,656 --> 00:46:33,352
Principle? The
heck with principle.

731
00:46:33,524 --> 00:46:36,926
They're destroying your reputation,
you don't even have one yet.

732
00:46:37,094 --> 00:46:39,855
Well, if he's not gonna do anything,
isn't there something you can do?

733
00:46:39,997 --> 00:46:42,262
- Yep.
- Well, what?

734
00:46:43,334 --> 00:46:44,962
I'm not telling you.

735
00:46:49,140 --> 00:46:52,702
I'm sorry, but I'm afraid seeing
Mr. Robinson and Mr. Reeves

736
00:46:52,877 --> 00:46:55,540
without an appointment is
absolutely out of the question.

737
00:46:55,713 --> 00:47:00,117
Well, just tell them that
I'm here, see what happens.

738
00:47:00,284 --> 00:47:01,877
Ben Matlock.

739
00:47:02,186 --> 00:47:03,654
They are in conference.

740
00:47:03,821 --> 00:47:07,588
Well, give it a shot.
What the heck?

741
00:47:13,497 --> 00:47:17,867
Yeah, I'm sorry to bother you,
but there's a Ben Matlock here,

742
00:47:18,035 --> 00:47:19,333
and he'd like to see you.

743
00:47:19,503 --> 00:47:22,302
I've already told
him that that's...

744
00:47:26,110 --> 00:47:27,738
All right.

745
00:47:33,117 --> 00:47:35,951
- This way, please.
- Mm-hm.

746
00:47:40,324 --> 00:47:42,384
- Well, it really is Ben Matlock.
- Yeah.

747
00:47:42,560 --> 00:47:43,920
- I'm John Robinson.
- Hello, John.

748
00:47:44,061 --> 00:47:45,962
- This is my partner, Mark Reeves.
- Mr. Reeves.

749
00:47:47,398 --> 00:47:49,230
Thank you, Doris.

750
00:47:50,000 --> 00:47:52,094
Well, I guess I'm
interrupting you fellows

751
00:47:52,269 --> 00:47:54,101
just as you're trying to decide

752
00:47:54,271 --> 00:47:56,467
what company you're
going to take over next.

753
00:47:56,640 --> 00:47:58,836
No, I was rather hoping
you had a hot tip for us.

754
00:47:59,009 --> 00:48:01,911
Oh, you could put what I
know about the stock market

755
00:48:02,079 --> 00:48:03,547
on the head of a pin.

756
00:48:03,714 --> 00:48:07,116
Now, I wanted to talk to
you about Cameron Ivers.

757
00:48:07,985 --> 00:48:09,817
- That's a terrible tragedy.
- Yeah.

758
00:48:09,987 --> 00:48:11,250
Yeah, yeah.

759
00:48:11,422 --> 00:48:12,981
He came to see you yesterday.

760
00:48:13,157 --> 00:48:15,251
- Yes. Yes, he did.
- Yeah.

761
00:48:15,426 --> 00:48:17,827
I talked with him
afterwards, and he said

762
00:48:17,995 --> 00:48:20,191
that there was a young
woman in here with you.

763
00:48:20,464 --> 00:48:23,229
The same young woman
that helped Scott Aston

764
00:48:23,401 --> 00:48:26,997
blackmail Cameron Ivers'
lawyer, Craig Browning.

765
00:48:28,038 --> 00:48:29,666
I'm afraid you've lost me.

766
00:48:29,840 --> 00:48:33,777
Ha, ha. Oh, well. Well,
at first when you arranged

767
00:48:33,944 --> 00:48:35,845
for this private detective

768
00:48:36,013 --> 00:48:39,609
to blackmail Craig Browning
into throwing Cameron Ivers' case,

769
00:48:39,784 --> 00:48:42,515
all you wanted was
Mr. Ivers to go to jail.

770
00:48:42,686 --> 00:48:44,882
But when that didn't work,
and then he came in here

771
00:48:45,055 --> 00:48:47,115
and confronted
you the way he did,

772
00:48:47,291 --> 00:48:53,197
well, you figured more
drastic action should be taken.

773
00:48:53,364 --> 00:48:54,388
How about it?

774
00:48:54,565 --> 00:48:57,194
We may be sharks in business,
but we do not kill people.

775
00:48:57,468 --> 00:48:59,596
And Cameron Ivers
was no threat to us.

776
00:48:59,770 --> 00:49:01,033
Oh, yes, he was.

777
00:49:01,305 --> 00:49:03,831
I don't know how
yet, but I know he was.

778
00:49:04,008 --> 00:49:06,034
I hate to burst your
bubble, Mr. Matlock,

779
00:49:06,210 --> 00:49:08,577
but Mark and I were
attending a black-tie fundraiser

780
00:49:08,746 --> 00:49:11,238
- at the Ritz Carlton last night.
- Oh.

781
00:49:11,682 --> 00:49:14,242
Well, how about the night
Scott Aston was murdered?

782
00:49:14,418 --> 00:49:16,717
Between 7 and 8.

783
00:49:16,887 --> 00:49:20,085
- What night was that?
- Say, that'd be... Let's see...

784
00:49:20,257 --> 00:49:21,623
Tuesday, the 3rd.

785
00:49:22,393 --> 00:49:24,593
- It was Tuesday.
- Playing racquetball at The Spa Club.

786
00:49:24,728 --> 00:49:26,162
With each other, I guess.

787
00:49:26,330 --> 00:49:28,424
- Yes.
- Yeah.

788
00:49:28,599 --> 00:49:32,832
Well, maybe this whole thing
is just one big coincidence.

789
00:49:33,003 --> 00:49:34,995
The blackmail, the girl,

790
00:49:35,172 --> 00:49:37,573
Mr. Ivers getting
run down like that.

791
00:49:37,741 --> 00:49:39,821
I assure you, Mr. Matlock,
that's exactly what it is.

792
00:49:39,977 --> 00:49:43,209
Mm-hm. Well,
thanks for your time.

793
00:49:43,380 --> 00:49:46,578
And, uh, now that
we've had our little talk,

794
00:49:46,750 --> 00:49:49,219
I sure hope nothing happens

795
00:49:49,386 --> 00:49:52,823
to that girl, Rachel
Bauer, don't you?

796
00:50:00,931 --> 00:50:03,992
Your friend Ben Matlock is
an alarmingly smart fellow.

797
00:50:04,168 --> 00:50:05,192
He's not my friend.

798
00:50:05,369 --> 00:50:07,736
And he only looks smart
compared to you two.

799
00:50:08,873 --> 00:50:10,068
What's that supposed to mean?

800
00:50:10,241 --> 00:50:12,767
Killing Cameron
Ivers was stupid.

801
00:50:12,943 --> 00:50:14,935
That's what it means.

802
00:50:15,479 --> 00:50:17,675
What makes you
think we'd kill him?

803
00:50:17,948 --> 00:50:20,179
The man had enemies
all over the world.

804
00:50:20,351 --> 00:50:22,786
One of whom happened to be you.

805
00:50:26,223 --> 00:50:28,249
All that stuff was in
Scott Aston's pockets?

806
00:50:28,425 --> 00:50:29,449
Yes, it was.

807
00:50:29,627 --> 00:50:30,959
- A real pack rat, huh?
- Yeah.

808
00:50:31,128 --> 00:50:35,190
I wonder why he went to Florida
the day before he was killed.

809
00:50:35,533 --> 00:50:36,899
What, you find something?

810
00:50:37,067 --> 00:50:38,831
Sack of peanuts.

811
00:50:39,003 --> 00:50:41,598
I can smell them
through the wrapper.

812
00:50:41,839 --> 00:50:43,865
- Fat. Just fat, see?
- Mm.

813
00:50:44,041 --> 00:50:45,407
Probably why they smell so good.

814
00:50:47,011 --> 00:50:49,537
Shame to let them
go to waste, though.

815
00:50:49,713 --> 00:50:52,376
Starvation in the world...

816
00:50:54,451 --> 00:50:56,044
Let's see what we got here.

817
00:50:56,220 --> 00:50:58,246
Uh, driver's
license, library card,

818
00:50:58,422 --> 00:51:01,881
ATM card, car
rental, video club...

819
00:51:02,359 --> 00:51:04,624
"Top of the Line Car Rental."

820
00:51:04,795 --> 00:51:08,732
They say if you use their credit card,
you can get a free rental sometime.

821
00:51:08,899 --> 00:51:10,231
Mm.

822
00:51:10,401 --> 00:51:13,064
If he rented a car from
them down in Florida,

823
00:51:13,237 --> 00:51:16,173
he'd have to leave a local address
and phone number, wouldn't he?

824
00:51:17,107 --> 00:51:18,131
Right.

825
00:51:18,309 --> 00:51:19,834
- Find out, will you?
- Okay.

826
00:51:20,010 --> 00:51:23,310
- Uh, I got to go home.
- What's over at the house?

827
00:51:23,480 --> 00:51:26,143
Uh, I got to meet a fellow

828
00:51:26,584 --> 00:51:28,644
and, uh, move some furniture.

829
00:51:32,723 --> 00:51:34,214
Come on, fight
it, fight it. Get up.

830
00:51:34,391 --> 00:51:35,711
Good, good, good,
keep breathing.

831
00:51:35,859 --> 00:51:37,521
Keep breathing.
Good, good, good.

832
00:51:37,695 --> 00:51:40,028
Attaboy, keep going.
Couple more, couple more.

833
00:51:40,197 --> 00:51:42,496
- Good job.
- My stomach's on fire.

834
00:51:42,666 --> 00:51:44,498
That's good, that's good.
That's what we want.

835
00:51:44,668 --> 00:51:46,227
Burn those abs, blast them.

836
00:51:46,403 --> 00:51:48,235
Oh! I can't do any more.

837
00:51:48,405 --> 00:51:49,964
All right, good job.

838
00:51:50,140 --> 00:51:52,507
You went to failure.
That's what we want.

839
00:51:52,676 --> 00:51:56,272
Okay, Mr. M., hop up.
We're gonna do some squats.

840
00:51:57,414 --> 00:52:00,475
- Some what?
- Squats. Watch.

841
00:52:00,651 --> 00:52:03,120
Three sets of 15 reps,
holding 20 pounds.

842
00:52:03,287 --> 00:52:05,586
Maybe we'll pyramid up
to 30. We'll see how it goes.

843
00:52:07,758 --> 00:52:10,956
You know, half the time, I don't
know if you're talking English.

844
00:52:12,296 --> 00:52:14,231
You're a riot, you know
that? Here, grab them.

845
00:52:14,598 --> 00:52:17,591
Oh, no more dumbbells.
Just make me sweat.

846
00:52:17,768 --> 00:52:20,203
That's something I do
real good. Make me sweat.

847
00:52:20,371 --> 00:52:22,966
The name of the
game is muscle mass.

848
00:52:23,140 --> 00:52:26,668
All this exercise and all I've
lost is a pound and a half.

849
00:52:26,844 --> 00:52:30,042
You have got to stop measuring
your progress by the scale.

850
00:52:30,214 --> 00:52:33,309
It's how you look and how you
feel, not how much you weigh.

851
00:52:33,484 --> 00:52:36,454
I hired you because
I need to lose this

852
00:52:36,620 --> 00:52:37,986
by the 23rd.

853
00:52:38,422 --> 00:52:42,450
Now, give it to me straight.
Is it possible? Yes or no?

854
00:52:42,860 --> 00:52:44,852
- Well, that depends.
- On what?

855
00:52:45,029 --> 00:52:47,260
On how you feel
about liposuction.

856
00:52:58,509 --> 00:53:03,914
So all Cain Mortuary does is
issue phony death certificates?

857
00:53:04,081 --> 00:53:05,549
It sure looks that way.

858
00:53:05,716 --> 00:53:09,016
So far I've located seven
people who are dead

859
00:53:09,186 --> 00:53:10,279
according to Cain Mortuary,

860
00:53:10,454 --> 00:53:12,753
but are, without a doubt, alive.

861
00:53:12,923 --> 00:53:16,826
They either live out
of town or out of state.

862
00:53:16,994 --> 00:53:18,656
They know they're
supposed to be dead?

863
00:53:18,829 --> 00:53:20,855
None of them had any
idea that they were dead,

864
00:53:21,031 --> 00:53:24,297
but their obituaries had
appeared in <i>The Atlanta Herald.</i>

865
00:53:24,468 --> 00:53:25,492
This is good.

866
00:53:25,669 --> 00:53:26,830
- This is good.
- Mm.

867
00:53:27,004 --> 00:53:28,004
Mm.

868
00:53:28,172 --> 00:53:30,505
I used to eat like that.

869
00:53:31,809 --> 00:53:33,675
Why'd you quit?

870
00:53:35,646 --> 00:53:37,012
Couldn't buy pants.

871
00:53:37,548 --> 00:53:40,108
What do you mean
you couldn't buy pants?

872
00:53:40,384 --> 00:53:44,981
I couldn't buy pants with
a 33-inch waist that fit.

873
00:53:46,056 --> 00:53:49,515
Well, no wonder.
Heh-heh. Look at the gut.

874
00:53:51,895 --> 00:53:55,195
Well, he's not so big.

875
00:53:56,667 --> 00:53:58,192
Get the tape. Ha-ha-ha.

876
00:53:59,002 --> 00:54:00,129
Dad.

877
00:54:00,871 --> 00:54:03,397
Well, anyway, um, I thought
the best way to find out

878
00:54:03,574 --> 00:54:05,736
if these fake death
certificates had anything to do

879
00:54:05,909 --> 00:54:08,469
with Scott Aston's murder would
be to get all the people together

880
00:54:08,612 --> 00:54:10,410
and find out if they have
anything in common.

881
00:54:10,581 --> 00:54:12,447
How are you gonna do that?

882
00:54:13,283 --> 00:54:15,650
Well, I have an idea.

883
00:54:15,819 --> 00:54:18,789
But it could be expensive.

884
00:54:28,866 --> 00:54:30,892
- I came on a 747.
- Really?

885
00:54:31,068 --> 00:54:32,161
Excuse me.

886
00:54:34,404 --> 00:54:35,895
Excuse me.

887
00:54:36,740 --> 00:54:38,606
Excuse me.

888
00:54:41,345 --> 00:54:42,779
Hi.

889
00:54:42,946 --> 00:54:44,812
We need to get started.
Welcome to Atlanta.

890
00:54:44,982 --> 00:54:48,612
Again, I'm Jerri Stone, and
this man over here is Billy Lewis.

891
00:54:48,786 --> 00:54:50,778
He's the one that
paid to fly you out here

892
00:54:50,954 --> 00:54:52,513
and put you up
at the nice hotel.

893
00:54:52,689 --> 00:54:54,453
- Hey.
- How you doing?

894
00:54:55,726 --> 00:54:58,423
That's okay. I did
it for my boy here.

895
00:54:58,595 --> 00:55:00,860
Now, as I explained to
all of you on the phone,

896
00:55:01,031 --> 00:55:03,796
I've reason to believe that you
all have something in common,

897
00:55:03,967 --> 00:55:05,959
and we're here to
discover what that might be.

898
00:55:06,537 --> 00:55:10,235
Okay, how many of you know
a man named John Robinson?

899
00:55:10,407 --> 00:55:12,706
- Never heard of him.
- No.

900
00:55:12,876 --> 00:55:15,277
- Uh, Mark Reeves?
- The guy who played Superman?

901
00:55:15,445 --> 00:55:18,904
- You're thinking of Steve Reeves.
- No, that was George Reeves.

902
00:55:19,082 --> 00:55:21,244
You mean Christopher Reeve.

903
00:55:21,418 --> 00:55:23,284
You guys don't know
what you're talking about.

904
00:55:23,453 --> 00:55:24,477
Okay. Okay, okay.

905
00:55:24,655 --> 00:55:27,557
Okay, how about a man
named Cameron Ivers?

906
00:55:27,724 --> 00:55:29,693
Hey, I've heard of him.

907
00:55:29,860 --> 00:55:31,726
I think I have.

908
00:55:31,895 --> 00:55:34,296
What about this? Have any of
you ever lived here in Atlanta?

909
00:55:34,464 --> 00:55:35,488
- No.
- No.

910
00:55:35,666 --> 00:55:38,431
- My grandson lived here once.
- When I was, like, 8.

911
00:55:38,602 --> 00:55:40,571
Where was everybody born?

912
00:55:40,737 --> 00:55:42,638
- Oklahoma City.
- Minnesota.

913
00:55:42,806 --> 00:55:44,206
You know, you
look really familiar.

914
00:55:44,341 --> 00:55:46,622
Weren't you on that cruise I
took last year to St. Thomas?

915
00:55:46,777 --> 00:55:47,801
Are you kidding?

916
00:55:47,978 --> 00:55:49,913
I throw up just looking
at pictures of boats.

917
00:55:50,080 --> 00:55:51,605
No, that wasn't me.

918
00:55:51,782 --> 00:55:55,048
Well, what religion
is everybody?

919
00:55:55,219 --> 00:55:57,279
I'm Episcopalian,

920
00:55:57,454 --> 00:55:59,094
but now I go to
Presbyterian church often.

921
00:55:59,256 --> 00:56:01,456
How many of you think Atlanta
would have won the pennant

922
00:56:01,592 --> 00:56:04,357
- if there hadn't been a strike?
- All right. Yeah, sure.

923
00:56:05,829 --> 00:56:07,821
- Yeah, yeah.
- Great.

924
00:56:07,998 --> 00:56:12,436
Well, guess I'm going to Miami.

925
00:56:17,374 --> 00:56:19,240
- Let me get that.
- No, I got it.

926
00:56:19,409 --> 00:56:21,889
- Well, let me drive you to the airport.
- I got a taxi coming.

927
00:56:22,045 --> 00:56:24,844
- Well, I'm a good driver.
- Fair.

928
00:56:25,015 --> 00:56:27,575
I'll take you for free.

929
00:56:28,619 --> 00:56:29,678
No, Billy.

930
00:56:29,853 --> 00:56:32,093
What's the matter with you?
"Free" is your favorite word.

931
00:56:32,256 --> 00:56:35,658
- You say it in ten different languages.
- It wouldn't wind up being free.

932
00:56:35,826 --> 00:56:38,125
You'd keep butting in
and trying to take over.

933
00:56:38,295 --> 00:56:41,026
Ben, Cliff's my only child.

934
00:56:41,198 --> 00:56:44,430
My sole heir, and he's
in trouble right now.

935
00:56:44,601 --> 00:56:46,695
Well, he's pretty smart.

936
00:56:46,870 --> 00:56:48,930
I know he doesn't
seem it sometimes,

937
00:56:49,106 --> 00:56:52,770
especially now with this
lawyer-client, can't-talk-about-it stuff,

938
00:56:53,243 --> 00:56:55,337
but I wanna help.

939
00:56:55,512 --> 00:56:56,980
I'll pay for the plane tickets.

940
00:56:58,448 --> 00:57:00,542
No, Billy.

941
00:57:01,618 --> 00:57:04,588
Come on, Ben. I'll
stay out of your way.

942
00:57:04,755 --> 00:57:07,190
I won't say a single word
the whole time, I promise.

943
00:57:07,357 --> 00:57:09,517
I'll just go with you and
hear what people have to say.

944
00:57:09,660 --> 00:57:13,062
- You'll butt in, Billy.
- You won't even know I'm there.

945
00:57:13,230 --> 00:57:16,530
I know, except for the
colorful way you're dressed.

946
00:57:16,700 --> 00:57:19,499
I'll buy you a colorful
suit. That way we'll blend.

947
00:57:19,670 --> 00:57:20,968
No, Billy.

948
00:57:21,405 --> 00:57:23,965
- Car, meals, hotel, I'll pay everything.
- No.

949
00:57:24,141 --> 00:57:26,406
- And anything else that comes up too.
- No.

950
00:57:26,576 --> 00:57:29,842
I'll buy presents for everybody
at the airport on the way home.

951
00:57:30,013 --> 00:57:33,506
Well, since you
put it that way...

952
00:57:34,685 --> 00:57:35,880
No.

953
00:57:52,869 --> 00:57:53,928
- Mr. Matlock.
- Yes.

954
00:57:54,104 --> 00:57:55,744
- Hi. Welcome to Miami Beach.
- Thank you.

955
00:57:55,872 --> 00:57:57,135
What can I do for you?

956
00:57:57,307 --> 00:58:00,402
Well, I called from Atlanta
about a man named Scott Aston.

957
00:58:00,577 --> 00:58:02,569
The Top of the Line
Car Rental company

958
00:58:02,746 --> 00:58:04,305
said he stayed here on the 2nd.

959
00:58:04,481 --> 00:58:05,574
- That's right.
- Yeah.

960
00:58:05,749 --> 00:58:08,446
- You said he was murdered?
- Yeah, the next day.

961
00:58:08,618 --> 00:58:11,747
You got a warrant that
allows me to look at his bill?

962
00:58:11,922 --> 00:58:13,447
Oh, yes.

963
00:58:17,427 --> 00:58:19,396
There you go.
It's pretty minimal.

964
00:58:19,563 --> 00:58:20,861
Sure is.

965
00:58:21,031 --> 00:58:22,591
Paid for one night,
and that's about it.

966
00:58:22,766 --> 00:58:24,394
No meals, no parking, no calls.

967
00:58:25,769 --> 00:58:28,432
Only charge was for a
one-page fax he received.

968
00:58:28,605 --> 00:58:30,801
Want me to see if we
still have a copy of it?

969
00:58:30,974 --> 00:58:33,239
You keep copies of
all the faxes you get?

970
00:58:33,410 --> 00:58:36,574
In this day and age, it pays
to make backups of everything.

971
00:58:36,747 --> 00:58:38,909
- I'll be right back.
- Okay.

972
00:58:52,362 --> 00:58:53,421
- Hi.
- Hi.

973
00:58:53,597 --> 00:58:55,122
- Chuck Ratner.
- Yeah.

974
00:58:55,298 --> 00:58:56,618
So you found the
place okay, huh?

975
00:58:56,767 --> 00:59:00,670
I just followed the
directions on that fax you sent

976
00:59:00,837 --> 00:59:02,738
to Scott Aston when
he came to see you.

977
00:59:02,906 --> 00:59:05,535
Oh, you hungry? My wife
just made some cookies.

978
00:59:05,709 --> 00:59:09,237
In fact, I can smell
them all the way out here.

979
00:59:09,413 --> 00:59:12,474
Oh, yeah. Oh,
I'd better not, I...

980
00:59:12,649 --> 00:59:15,517
I got my 40th college
reunion coming up,

981
00:59:15,685 --> 00:59:17,711
and I'm trying to lose
a little weight. Heh-heh.

982
00:59:17,888 --> 00:59:19,528
Well, why don't we
just take a walk, then?

983
00:59:19,656 --> 00:59:23,991
- That way I can show off the place.
- That sounds good. Yeah.

984
00:59:26,029 --> 00:59:28,521
Boy, this is a pretty place.

985
00:59:28,965 --> 00:59:32,060
- I bet you like living here.
- Oh, yes, sir.

986
00:59:32,235 --> 00:59:35,228
So you said you had some
questions about Scott Aston?

987
00:59:35,405 --> 00:59:38,466
Yeah, I wanted to know why
he came all the way down here

988
00:59:38,642 --> 00:59:40,201
from Atlanta to see you.

989
00:59:40,377 --> 00:59:41,902
I needed a private investigator.

990
00:59:42,079 --> 00:59:45,072
- I was thinking about hiring him.
- To do what?

991
00:59:45,248 --> 00:59:47,740
Mind my asking why
you wanna know?

992
00:59:48,185 --> 00:59:50,814
He was murdered the next day.

993
00:59:52,556 --> 00:59:55,025
- He's dead?
- Yeah.

994
00:59:55,392 --> 01:00:00,262
I'm trying to find out who
killed him and, you know, why.

995
01:00:02,399 --> 01:00:04,129
My wife and I have two children.

996
01:00:04,301 --> 01:00:06,532
My youngest is a little
boy who's 5 years old,

997
01:00:06,703 --> 01:00:09,434
and he was born
with severe disabilities.

998
01:00:09,606 --> 01:00:13,202
Now, we love him dearly,
but it's hard caring for him.

999
01:00:13,376 --> 01:00:16,278
It's hard on my wife and
me, it's hard on our daughter.

1000
01:00:16,446 --> 01:00:18,506
It's hard on our marriage.

1001
01:00:18,682 --> 01:00:19,706
I imagine.

1002
01:00:19,883 --> 01:00:24,548
Anyway, somehow I got it in my
brain that my wife was having an affair,

1003
01:00:24,721 --> 01:00:28,852
so I flew Scott Aston down
here to find out if it was true.

1004
01:00:29,025 --> 01:00:32,885
Oh, you wanted him
to, uh... To spy on her?

1005
01:00:33,497 --> 01:00:36,433
Well, I just wanted to know.

1006
01:00:36,900 --> 01:00:38,493
But talking to him was...

1007
01:00:38,668 --> 01:00:40,569
Turned out to be kind
of therapeutic for me.

1008
01:00:40,737 --> 01:00:45,004
I finally realized that I was
probably just overreacting,

1009
01:00:45,175 --> 01:00:47,007
so I sent him home

1010
01:00:47,177 --> 01:00:49,840
and told him I'd call him
after I thought things over.

1011
01:00:50,013 --> 01:00:52,312
Well, why him? Why didn't
you get somebody local?

1012
01:00:52,482 --> 01:00:53,973
Well, a friend of mine used him,

1013
01:00:54,151 --> 01:00:56,814
and I wasn't about to
use the <i>Yellow Pages.</i>

1014
01:00:57,754 --> 01:00:59,746
- Did you call him back?
- No.

1015
01:00:59,923 --> 01:01:02,449
I decided I was overreacting.

1016
01:01:03,126 --> 01:01:05,721
Now, that is what
we call a preschool.

1017
01:01:06,229 --> 01:01:07,857
Come on, giddap. Come on.

1018
01:01:08,031 --> 01:01:09,631
It's where we work
out all our yearlings.

1019
01:01:09,799 --> 01:01:11,131
Mm.

1020
01:01:12,402 --> 01:01:14,132
How long you had this place?

1021
01:01:14,304 --> 01:01:17,240
Well, let's see,
I retired in '92.

1022
01:01:17,407 --> 01:01:20,104
It's been going on
about three years.

1023
01:01:20,277 --> 01:01:22,337
You're awful young.

1024
01:01:22,512 --> 01:01:24,674
How could you retire?
How could you afford it?

1025
01:01:24,848 --> 01:01:27,613
Well, I was in the right
business at the right time.

1026
01:01:27,784 --> 01:01:29,218
Computer software.

1027
01:01:29,386 --> 01:01:32,356
Oh, I made more money
than I knew what to do with.

1028
01:01:32,522 --> 01:01:35,356
But when our son was born, I...

1029
01:01:35,525 --> 01:01:38,017
- I decided enough was enough.
- Yeah.

1030
01:01:38,195 --> 01:01:39,686
I just... I wanted
to stay at home

1031
01:01:39,863 --> 01:01:44,267
and make sure that he got
off to the best possible start.

1032
01:01:44,434 --> 01:01:47,563
- Well, that's good. Smart, smart.
- Come on. Giddap.

1033
01:01:47,737 --> 01:01:50,400
Well, you've answered all
my questions. I appreciate it.

1034
01:01:50,574 --> 01:01:52,770
Well, don't you wanna
see the rest of the farm?

1035
01:01:52,943 --> 01:01:56,812
Well, no. Heh. Horses
and I don't really...

1036
01:01:58,348 --> 01:02:01,182
I'll get something
to wipe that off.

1037
01:02:01,651 --> 01:02:03,449
Scott Aston went
all the way to Florida

1038
01:02:03,620 --> 01:02:05,851
to talk to some
potential client, that's all?

1039
01:02:06,022 --> 01:02:10,221
<i>Well, that squares with the
mileage on the car he rented.</i>

1040
01:02:10,393 --> 01:02:12,953
<i>He drove from the
airport to the hotel,</i>

1041
01:02:13,129 --> 01:02:15,030
drove down to the farm.

1042
01:02:15,198 --> 01:02:18,191
Drove back to the
hotel, spent the night,

1043
01:02:18,368 --> 01:02:20,803
drove to the
airport and flew out.

1044
01:02:20,971 --> 01:02:22,337
<i>So it's a dead end, huh?</i>

1045
01:02:22,505 --> 01:02:24,804
Well, I don't know.

1046
01:02:24,975 --> 01:02:27,274
See what you can
find out about this fella.

1047
01:02:27,444 --> 01:02:29,913
Chuck Ratner's his name.

1048
01:02:30,080 --> 01:02:32,845
He owns a horse farm
down near Hialeah.

1049
01:02:33,216 --> 01:02:36,311
<i>He used to work for some kind
of computer software company.</i>

1050
01:02:36,486 --> 01:02:40,014
- Ratner, right. I'll do that. Pop...
- You should have taken me with you.

1051
01:02:40,190 --> 01:02:42,819
<i>I would have gotten something
out of the guy right there and then.</i>

1052
01:02:42,993 --> 01:02:44,359
Pop.

1053
01:02:44,527 --> 01:02:47,156
Yeah, I'm sorry. So when
are you coming home?

1054
01:02:47,330 --> 01:02:50,960
<i>Well, the plane gets in at 4:05,</i>

1055
01:02:51,768 --> 01:02:54,397
but I'm not coming
straight home.

1056
01:02:54,571 --> 01:02:58,906
Uh, I gotta make a stop.

1057
01:03:08,818 --> 01:03:11,549
Can't you do something?

1058
01:03:11,721 --> 01:03:14,452
I think maybe you came
to the wrong place, Ben.

1059
01:03:14,624 --> 01:03:16,092
You don't need a tailor.

1060
01:03:16,259 --> 01:03:18,990
- You need an engineer. Ha, ha!
- Oh, come on, Marv.

1061
01:03:19,162 --> 01:03:22,655
Everybody puts on a little
weight as they get older.

1062
01:03:23,933 --> 01:03:25,128
What are you doing?

1063
01:03:25,302 --> 01:03:26,861
I'm holding my stomach in.

1064
01:03:27,037 --> 01:03:29,199
- You are?
- Yes.

1065
01:03:29,539 --> 01:03:31,633
- Where you putting it?
- Come on, Marv.

1066
01:03:31,808 --> 01:03:34,243
Oh, don't worry. I'm just
making a little small talk

1067
01:03:34,411 --> 01:03:36,039
while I get a lay
of the land here.

1068
01:03:36,212 --> 01:03:38,010
Can't you just let it out some?

1069
01:03:38,181 --> 01:03:40,173
Well, there's not
enough material.

1070
01:03:40,350 --> 01:03:43,047
Well, maybe if I can find
some to match the coat

1071
01:03:43,219 --> 01:03:47,213
and put it here in the back
and sort of widen it out a little bit.

1072
01:03:47,390 --> 01:03:50,053
Move the buttons over
here on the trousers

1073
01:03:50,226 --> 01:03:54,129
and fix the zipper,
well, then, you'll be set.

1074
01:03:54,631 --> 01:03:57,226
Thanks, Marv. I
thought you could do it.

1075
01:04:00,136 --> 01:04:02,071
I can fix that too.

1076
01:04:11,448 --> 01:04:15,715
Okay, I know you're all a little
anxious to get home. Cookie?

1077
01:04:15,885 --> 01:04:17,376
This is worse than jury duty.

1078
01:04:17,554 --> 01:04:19,284
I'd like to see my grandkids.

1079
01:04:19,456 --> 01:04:21,823
Everybody's got something to do.

1080
01:04:21,991 --> 01:04:24,859
Please, whatever it is you
people have in common,

1081
01:04:25,028 --> 01:04:26,724
two men may be
dead because of it.

1082
01:04:26,896 --> 01:04:28,797
Now, we've gotta
figure this out.

1083
01:04:31,434 --> 01:04:33,130
Hello, everybody.

1084
01:04:33,303 --> 01:04:35,772
Just keep talking to each
other. Talk about, uh, school,

1085
01:04:35,939 --> 01:04:39,637
siblings, hobbies,
clubs, jobs, something.

1086
01:04:39,809 --> 01:04:41,937
You've gotta have
something in common.

1087
01:04:42,112 --> 01:04:43,842
- Hi.
- Hi. Where's Ben?

1088
01:04:44,013 --> 01:04:45,641
He went over to the courthouse.

1089
01:04:45,815 --> 01:04:47,511
Well, I've been
doing a little research.

1090
01:04:47,684 --> 01:04:49,653
You know the guy he
went to see down in Florida?

1091
01:04:49,819 --> 01:04:51,117
- Uh, Chuck Ratner?
- Yeah.

1092
01:04:51,287 --> 01:04:54,951
Well, the guy said that he worked
for a computer software company.

1093
01:04:55,125 --> 01:04:56,149
He lied.

1094
01:04:56,326 --> 01:04:57,954
He worked for Roger
Herman's company,

1095
01:04:58,128 --> 01:05:00,495
- Ashfield Life Insurance.
- Ashfield Life?

1096
01:05:00,663 --> 01:05:01,663
- Yeah.
- Ashfield Life.

1097
01:05:01,831 --> 01:05:02,831
I know that company.

1098
01:05:02,999 --> 01:05:05,696
I almost bought a policy
from them. Then I discovered

1099
01:05:05,869 --> 01:05:08,270
I could get the same thing
for less somewhere else.

1100
01:05:08,438 --> 01:05:10,737
- Me too.
- At least they give you a free pen

1101
01:05:10,907 --> 01:05:12,500
when you sign up an application.

1102
01:05:12,675 --> 01:05:13,734
Hold the phone.

1103
01:05:13,910 --> 01:05:16,277
How many of you, at
one time or another,

1104
01:05:16,446 --> 01:05:19,780
have applied for life
insurance from Ashfield Life?

1105
01:05:19,949 --> 01:05:21,076
- Yeah, I did.
- I did.

1106
01:05:21,251 --> 01:05:23,049
- I did.
- I did.

1107
01:05:36,433 --> 01:05:39,597
Oh, good, good, good.
The gang's all here.

1108
01:05:39,769 --> 01:05:42,170
This better be as
urgent as you indicated.

1109
01:05:42,338 --> 01:05:43,704
Oh, it's pretty complicated.

1110
01:05:43,873 --> 01:05:48,038
- Maybe everybody better sit down.
- Just get to the point.

1111
01:05:48,211 --> 01:05:49,804
Okay.

1112
01:05:50,079 --> 01:05:52,776
I know what you've
all been up to.

1113
01:05:53,616 --> 01:05:56,916
And I know that Mr. Herman here

1114
01:05:57,086 --> 01:06:00,682
has been using information
on application forms of people

1115
01:06:00,857 --> 01:06:04,726
who decided not to buy
Ashfield Life insurance.

1116
01:06:05,762 --> 01:06:07,492
They filled out the forms,

1117
01:06:07,664 --> 01:06:09,030
but they didn't buy.

1118
01:06:09,199 --> 01:06:13,603
And he used the information
on those applications

1119
01:06:13,770 --> 01:06:17,298
to issue a lot of phony
life-insurance policies.

1120
01:06:17,474 --> 01:06:18,999
Now, why in the
world would I do that?

1121
01:06:19,175 --> 01:06:20,438
- Money.
- That's ridiculous.

1122
01:06:20,610 --> 01:06:23,205
How can I make any money if
there's no one to pay the premiums?

1123
01:06:23,379 --> 01:06:25,939
Well, according to the state
insurance commissioner,

1124
01:06:26,115 --> 01:06:28,812
you could sell those
phony-insurance policies

1125
01:06:28,985 --> 01:06:30,453
to another company.

1126
01:06:30,620 --> 01:06:32,987
You'd remain the administrator

1127
01:06:33,156 --> 01:06:36,752
and you could pay the
premiums for, oh, a year or two,

1128
01:06:36,926 --> 01:06:41,455
and then one by one
issue fake death certificates.

1129
01:06:41,631 --> 01:06:44,362
That way you'd collect
all the death benefits

1130
01:06:44,534 --> 01:06:48,096
from the second insurance company,
keeping all the money for yourself.

1131
01:06:48,271 --> 01:06:49,271
Huh?

1132
01:06:49,772 --> 01:06:52,970
Of course, in order to
issue fake death certificates,

1133
01:06:53,142 --> 01:06:55,543
you'd have to own a mortuary.

1134
01:06:55,712 --> 01:06:57,078
Which you do.

1135
01:06:57,413 --> 01:06:59,405
Cain Mortuary.

1136
01:07:00,884 --> 01:07:02,614
Am I to understand...?

1137
01:07:02,785 --> 01:07:05,846
Am I to understand that you've
discussed all of this already

1138
01:07:06,022 --> 01:07:07,923
with the state commissioner?

1139
01:07:08,091 --> 01:07:09,115
Oh, yeah, yeah.

1140
01:07:09,626 --> 01:07:12,221
Yeah. He said it's a
very ingenious idea.

1141
01:07:12,395 --> 01:07:14,523
Highly illegal but
very ingenious.

1142
01:07:14,697 --> 01:07:16,393
He wants to talk to you.

1143
01:07:16,566 --> 01:07:19,695
Oh, and, uh, since
the partnership

1144
01:07:19,869 --> 01:07:23,931
of Mr. Robinson and
Mr. Reeves owns the bulk

1145
01:07:24,107 --> 01:07:26,872
of Ashfield Insurance stock,

1146
01:07:27,043 --> 01:07:28,739
outside, of course,
of Mr. Herman,

1147
01:07:29,245 --> 01:07:32,738
I also spoke with the Securities
and Exchange Commission.

1148
01:07:32,916 --> 01:07:35,909
Wait a minute. Ahem, we
bought that stock on good faith.

1149
01:07:36,085 --> 01:07:40,045
If what you're saying
is true, it's news to us.

1150
01:07:40,223 --> 01:07:42,920
Well, maybe you didn't
know about the fraud

1151
01:07:43,092 --> 01:07:44,390
when you bought the stock,

1152
01:07:44,561 --> 01:07:46,257
but you found out about it.

1153
01:07:46,429 --> 01:07:49,763
And that's why Mr. Ivers
was such a threat to you.

1154
01:07:50,300 --> 01:07:54,670
The... According to the
agreement, which you signed,

1155
01:07:54,837 --> 01:07:59,832
you were not permitted to
sell that stock for five months.

1156
01:08:00,543 --> 01:08:04,275
And if in that time
Mr. Ivers got interested,

1157
01:08:04,447 --> 01:08:07,679
started sniffing
around, found the fraud,

1158
01:08:07,850 --> 01:08:10,046
Ashfield Life would
be out of business.

1159
01:08:10,219 --> 01:08:15,851
And you'd be out,
what, $300 million?

1160
01:08:16,659 --> 01:08:18,958
And so to be sure

1161
01:08:19,462 --> 01:08:21,488
that Mr. Ivers
was out of the way

1162
01:08:22,131 --> 01:08:24,600
for at least five months,

1163
01:08:25,168 --> 01:08:28,036
you made sure he
had plenty to drink

1164
01:08:28,204 --> 01:08:29,638
at Mr. Robinson's party.

1165
01:08:29,806 --> 01:08:32,241
Remember Mr. Robinson's party?

1166
01:08:33,009 --> 01:08:37,572
And then you hired
somebody to shove a bum,

1167
01:08:37,747 --> 01:08:40,046
that nobody would
ever remember anyway,

1168
01:08:40,216 --> 01:08:42,879
out in front of his car when
he was on his way home.

1169
01:08:43,219 --> 01:08:46,621
And then you hired that
investigator, Scott Aston,

1170
01:08:46,789 --> 01:08:48,655
to blackmail his lawyer

1171
01:08:48,825 --> 01:08:53,024
and to throw in his case so
Mr. Ivers would wind up in jail.

1172
01:08:53,196 --> 01:08:59,358
But... But then, the
investigator, Aston,

1173
01:08:59,969 --> 01:09:02,438
he got curious about the fraud.

1174
01:09:05,074 --> 01:09:07,737
And he wound up in Florida,

1175
01:09:07,910 --> 01:09:11,142
talking to an
ex-Ashfield employee,

1176
01:09:11,314 --> 01:09:13,647
a fella named Chuck
Ratner, remember him,

1177
01:09:13,816 --> 01:09:17,048
about these fake
life-insurance policies.

1178
01:09:17,220 --> 01:09:18,745
The upshot of it

1179
01:09:19,689 --> 01:09:21,385
was you knew you
had to do something

1180
01:09:21,557 --> 01:09:22,581
about Scott Aston.

1181
01:09:22,759 --> 01:09:24,523
So one of you went
over to his hotel room

1182
01:09:24,694 --> 01:09:26,788
and hit him over the
head and killed him.

1183
01:09:26,963 --> 01:09:29,956
I repeat, John and I
played racquetball that night

1184
01:09:30,133 --> 01:09:31,999
from 6 to 8 at The Spa Club.

1185
01:09:32,168 --> 01:09:34,569
And I was in a meeting
with Chuck Ratner.

1186
01:09:34,737 --> 01:09:37,002
He came to see me
the day after Scott Aston

1187
01:09:37,173 --> 01:09:39,074
went to see him in Florida.

1188
01:09:39,242 --> 01:09:41,040
Yeah, I know. I
called him this morning

1189
01:09:41,210 --> 01:09:44,180
after I realized he had lied
to me the last time I saw him.

1190
01:09:44,347 --> 01:09:46,077
I hate it when people
do that, don't you?

1191
01:09:46,783 --> 01:09:50,151
Any other wild accusations
you'd like to hurl in our direction?

1192
01:09:50,787 --> 01:09:52,585
No, that about does it.

1193
01:09:53,056 --> 01:09:56,049
I'd talk to my
lawyers, gentlemen.

1194
01:09:56,693 --> 01:09:58,787
I'll see you in court.

1195
01:10:13,710 --> 01:10:15,645
- Hey, Cliff.
- Oh, hi, Craig.

1196
01:10:15,812 --> 01:10:17,452
Can I talk to you for
a second in private?

1197
01:10:17,580 --> 01:10:19,208
Yeah, sure.

1198
01:10:19,382 --> 01:10:20,441
I'll meet you inside.

1199
01:10:20,616 --> 01:10:23,142
Oh, thanks. Uh, yeah, what's up?

1200
01:10:23,319 --> 01:10:26,983
Look, forget all that lawyer-client
privileged information stuff, okay?

1201
01:10:27,657 --> 01:10:29,337
- Oh, no, no, no, I...
- No, no, I mean it.

1202
01:10:29,492 --> 01:10:31,484
You tell the police or
whomever else you need to

1203
01:10:31,661 --> 01:10:34,141
that I was being blackmailed
because I was cheating on my wife.

1204
01:10:34,297 --> 01:10:36,596
It's okay. Whatever
happens, I can deal with it.

1205
01:10:38,234 --> 01:10:39,930
Okay.

1206
01:10:41,003 --> 01:10:42,528
- Oh, you got guts.
- Heh-heh.

1207
01:10:43,639 --> 01:10:45,733
Cliff, wait. Um...

1208
01:10:48,044 --> 01:10:50,309
I don't have guts.

1209
01:10:50,780 --> 01:10:53,443
The police called my house
yesterday and talked to my wife.

1210
01:10:54,050 --> 01:10:58,112
She knows everything
and she wants a divorce.

1211
01:10:58,287 --> 01:11:00,415
What about the election?

1212
01:11:00,590 --> 01:11:02,855
She doesn't care whether
she wins or loses anymore.

1213
01:11:03,025 --> 01:11:04,960
She's pretty mad.

1214
01:11:06,162 --> 01:11:08,324
I wanted you to think that
I was taking the high road

1215
01:11:08,498 --> 01:11:12,799
because I do it so infrequently.

1216
01:11:14,070 --> 01:11:16,403
You're the one
with the guts, pal.

1217
01:11:16,973 --> 01:11:18,202
Good luck in there.

1218
01:11:19,976 --> 01:11:22,070
I'll be pulling for you.

1219
01:11:23,913 --> 01:11:25,472
Thanks.

1220
01:11:33,422 --> 01:11:36,586
I understand the
decedent, Scott Aston,

1221
01:11:36,759 --> 01:11:41,254
had a talk with you last month
the day before he was murdered,

1222
01:11:41,430 --> 01:11:43,456
on your horse farm
down there in Florida.

1223
01:11:43,633 --> 01:11:45,727
- Is that right?
- Yes, it is.

1224
01:11:45,902 --> 01:11:47,200
What did he wanna talk about?

1225
01:11:49,005 --> 01:11:50,405
He, uh...

1226
01:11:51,174 --> 01:11:52,574
He suspected

1227
01:11:52,742 --> 01:11:56,042
that Ashfield Life Insurance
Company was creating

1228
01:11:56,212 --> 01:11:59,546
fake life-insurance policies,
selling them to other companies

1229
01:12:00,016 --> 01:12:02,016
in order to defraud them
out of millions of dollars

1230
01:12:02,185 --> 01:12:03,209
in death benefits.

1231
01:12:05,288 --> 01:12:06,881
The last time I talked with you,

1232
01:12:07,056 --> 01:12:08,922
you told me something
else altogether.

1233
01:12:09,091 --> 01:12:11,560
Yes, sir. And I
apologize for that, sir.

1234
01:12:11,727 --> 01:12:13,992
- This is the truth. Yes, sir.
- This is the truth.

1235
01:12:14,163 --> 01:12:15,961
Well, why'd he come to you?

1236
01:12:16,132 --> 01:12:18,067
Because I used to work
at Ashfield Life, I guess.

1237
01:12:18,234 --> 01:12:19,354
Well, when you worked there,

1238
01:12:19,502 --> 01:12:23,530
did you know that fraudulent policies
were being sold to other companies?

1239
01:12:23,706 --> 01:12:24,901
I suspected it.

1240
01:12:25,074 --> 01:12:28,567
Is that why you quit
or, should I say, retired?

1241
01:12:28,744 --> 01:12:29,837
Yes.

1242
01:12:30,012 --> 01:12:33,073
And you retired with a very
generous pension, didn't you?

1243
01:12:34,050 --> 01:12:37,248
You mean, was I being
paid to keep quiet? No.

1244
01:12:38,120 --> 01:12:40,612
But you did keep quiet.

1245
01:12:41,724 --> 01:12:45,024
My son is
developmentally disabled.

1246
01:12:45,194 --> 01:12:48,164
Roger Herman, the
president of Ashfield Life,

1247
01:12:48,331 --> 01:12:50,698
has always shown nothing
but the utmost concern

1248
01:12:50,867 --> 01:12:53,701
and compassion for
my son and my family.

1249
01:12:53,870 --> 01:12:56,965
I didn't report my
suspicions because I...

1250
01:12:57,139 --> 01:12:58,607
I felt I owed him.

1251
01:12:59,041 --> 01:13:01,101
What did you tell Scott Aston?

1252
01:13:01,277 --> 01:13:04,441
Nothing. I told him I didn't
know what he was talking about.

1253
01:13:04,614 --> 01:13:07,209
- What did he do?
- He got angry and left.

1254
01:13:07,383 --> 01:13:10,046
- What did you do?
- Called Mr. Herman.

1255
01:13:10,219 --> 01:13:13,383
He didn't wanna talk on the phone,
so I flew up to Atlanta the next evening

1256
01:13:13,556 --> 01:13:16,082
and then I met him
at his club for drinks.

1257
01:13:16,259 --> 01:13:18,785
The next evening
would have been the 3rd,

1258
01:13:18,961 --> 01:13:20,190
the night of the murder.

1259
01:13:21,330 --> 01:13:24,790
Yes, yes. And I was...
I was with Mr. Herman

1260
01:13:24,967 --> 01:13:29,667
from about 6:45 to
about 8:30 that night.

1261
01:13:29,839 --> 01:13:31,967
Mm-hm, mm-hm.

1262
01:13:32,141 --> 01:13:37,512
Well, uh, during your
meeting with Mr. Aston,

1263
01:13:37,680 --> 01:13:41,617
did, uh, he mention Cliff Lewis?

1264
01:13:41,784 --> 01:13:43,980
- Who?
- The defendant.

1265
01:13:44,320 --> 01:13:47,051
Oh. No, he didn't. Never.

1266
01:13:47,223 --> 01:13:48,919
Did he mention Craig Browning?

1267
01:13:49,091 --> 01:13:51,151
- No.
- How about Cameron Ivers?

1268
01:13:51,327 --> 01:13:54,058
- Never.
- Did he give you the impression

1269
01:13:54,230 --> 01:13:56,825
that since he wasn't gonna
get anything out of you

1270
01:13:56,999 --> 01:14:00,492
about the fraud going
on at Ashfield Life,

1271
01:14:00,670 --> 01:14:02,002
he was gonna let
the matter drop?

1272
01:14:02,171 --> 01:14:03,696
He told me that, uh...

1273
01:14:03,873 --> 01:14:07,275
He said something like he
was gonna keep rattling cages

1274
01:14:07,443 --> 01:14:11,312
until either the truth or
somebody's head fell out in his lap.

1275
01:14:12,415 --> 01:14:15,647
And less than 24 hours
later, he was dead.

1276
01:14:15,818 --> 01:14:17,150
No more questions.

1277
01:14:19,855 --> 01:14:26,056
Well, it seems
Mr. Herman has an alibi.

1278
01:14:27,730 --> 01:14:29,596
One of them?

1279
01:14:40,309 --> 01:14:42,335
There they are, right on time.

1280
01:14:42,511 --> 01:14:44,104
They play racquetball
between 6 and 8

1281
01:14:44,280 --> 01:14:46,249
every Tuesday
and Thursday night,

1282
01:14:46,415 --> 01:14:49,180
and they have for
the past four years.

1283
01:14:49,652 --> 01:14:52,884
- Can I see them leaving?
- Yeah, right there.

1284
01:14:54,190 --> 01:14:55,681
There.

1285
01:14:55,858 --> 01:14:58,623
Twenty-twenty-two.
Twenty-two minutes after 8.

1286
01:14:58,794 --> 01:14:59,818
Good-looking guys.

1287
01:14:59,996 --> 01:15:02,465
Too bad one of them's so weird
and the other one's so married.

1288
01:15:03,933 --> 01:15:05,094
Well, thanks.

1289
01:15:05,267 --> 01:15:07,532
Would you mind if I
took a look around?

1290
01:15:07,703 --> 01:15:09,296
Sure.

1291
01:15:16,712 --> 01:15:18,476
Hi, sir. Have you
ever seen these men?

1292
01:15:18,647 --> 01:15:20,047
No.

1293
01:15:22,018 --> 01:15:23,042
Same as always.

1294
01:15:23,219 --> 01:15:25,211
Gentlemen, do you
know of a John Robinson?

1295
01:15:25,388 --> 01:15:26,981
- No.
- No.

1296
01:15:31,927 --> 01:15:33,953
Oh, what the heck.

1297
01:15:36,399 --> 01:15:38,732
So how many minutes
did you end up playing?

1298
01:15:39,769 --> 01:15:41,260
Really?

1299
01:15:41,437 --> 01:15:43,201
Hey, buddy.

1300
01:15:43,372 --> 01:15:44,601
Excuse me.

1301
01:15:44,774 --> 01:15:46,970
Female coming through.
Hide them if you got them.

1302
01:15:47,143 --> 01:15:49,703
- Whoa! Hello!
- I'm sorry, I gotta ask you a question.

1303
01:15:49,879 --> 01:15:51,006
You know these two guys?

1304
01:15:57,720 --> 01:15:59,416
Never mind.

1305
01:16:01,457 --> 01:16:04,450
You should dry your
hair. You'll catch a cold.

1306
01:16:05,094 --> 01:16:09,088
Yeah, well, the wet look's in, lady.
Hair dryers went out with perms.

1307
01:16:09,265 --> 01:16:10,893
Hey!

1308
01:16:17,173 --> 01:16:19,768
- I'm back. One more time.
- Whoa!

1309
01:16:19,942 --> 01:16:21,535
Cover them up.

1310
01:16:34,156 --> 01:16:37,718
Here you are entering the club
two weeks before the murder.

1311
01:16:39,095 --> 01:16:43,362
And here you are
leaving the club.

1312
01:16:44,133 --> 01:16:48,468
And here you are entering the
club one week before the murder.

1313
01:16:48,637 --> 01:16:53,371
And here you are
leaving the club.

1314
01:16:54,143 --> 01:16:55,873
Now, in those pictures,

1315
01:16:56,045 --> 01:16:58,640
when you entered the club,
was your hair wet or dry?

1316
01:16:58,814 --> 01:17:00,009
It's dry.

1317
01:17:00,316 --> 01:17:02,683
How about when
you left the club?

1318
01:17:02,852 --> 01:17:04,184
- Wet.
- How come?

1319
01:17:05,221 --> 01:17:07,213
Well, we take
showers after we play.

1320
01:17:07,389 --> 01:17:09,381
How come you don't dry?

1321
01:17:10,192 --> 01:17:11,751
The club doesn't
provide hair dryers.

1322
01:17:11,927 --> 01:17:15,193
Oh, so you just towel dry
it and leave it a little wet.

1323
01:17:15,364 --> 01:17:17,026
That's fashionable.

1324
01:17:17,199 --> 01:17:20,727
Now, here you are entering
the club January 3rd,

1325
01:17:20,903 --> 01:17:22,132
the night of the murder.

1326
01:17:23,506 --> 01:17:26,999
And here you are
leaving the club.

1327
01:17:27,576 --> 01:17:31,172
Now, how could that be?

1328
01:17:31,347 --> 01:17:34,943
You left the club with dry hair.

1329
01:17:35,484 --> 01:17:37,385
How could that be?

1330
01:17:37,786 --> 01:17:39,721
Well, um...

1331
01:17:41,357 --> 01:17:43,292
Oh, I know. That was
the night I hurt my knee.

1332
01:17:43,459 --> 01:17:46,520
We, uh, only played for about an
hour, and, uh, then we had a drink.

1333
01:17:46,695 --> 01:17:49,756
There's no record
of your buying a drink

1334
01:17:49,932 --> 01:17:52,527
or anything else at
the club that night.

1335
01:17:52,701 --> 01:17:55,535
You rented a
court, and that's it.

1336
01:17:55,704 --> 01:17:58,003
Only you didn't play
racquetball that night, did you?

1337
01:17:58,174 --> 01:18:01,770
You or Mr. Reeves or both
slipped out the back door,

1338
01:18:01,944 --> 01:18:05,608
went over to Mr. Aston's
hotel room, killed him,

1339
01:18:06,081 --> 01:18:08,448
slipped back in the club so
it'd look like you had an alibi.

1340
01:18:08,617 --> 01:18:11,109
- That's not true.
- It's already been established

1341
01:18:11,287 --> 01:18:13,882
that you stood to lose
hundreds of millions of dollars

1342
01:18:14,056 --> 01:18:18,255
if he lived to tell about the
fraud going on at Ashfield Life.

1343
01:18:18,427 --> 01:18:19,918
Right, Mr. Robinson?

1344
01:18:28,037 --> 01:18:30,268
I need to talk to my attorney.

1345
01:18:30,439 --> 01:18:32,169
Sounds like a good idea to me.

1346
01:18:33,042 --> 01:18:36,308
This court's in recess
till 9 a.m. tomorrow.

1347
01:18:37,012 --> 01:18:40,346
And because we are so successful,
people who are eager to get rich

1348
01:18:40,516 --> 01:18:43,486
try to do so by finding out
about our investment plans

1349
01:18:43,652 --> 01:18:45,985
and then acting on
them even before we do.

1350
01:18:46,155 --> 01:18:48,624
Our phones have been
tapped, our employees bribed,

1351
01:18:48,791 --> 01:18:50,384
our cars have been followed.

1352
01:18:50,559 --> 01:18:52,892
Secrecy has become an
absolute necessity for us,

1353
01:18:53,062 --> 01:18:54,860
and not just out
of self-interest.

1354
01:18:55,030 --> 01:18:57,124
- Federal law demands it.
- Federal law.

1355
01:18:57,299 --> 01:19:00,758
When do you get so law-abiding?
You've been indicted for fraud.

1356
01:19:00,936 --> 01:19:03,838
Yes, but we're not murderers.

1357
01:19:04,273 --> 01:19:06,799
You were right. We did use
the sports club as a cover.

1358
01:19:06,976 --> 01:19:08,976
But were we to say where
we really went that night,

1359
01:19:09,144 --> 01:19:12,114
the SEC could very easily consider
it a violation of insider trading laws.

1360
01:19:12,281 --> 01:19:13,544
Wait a minute.

1361
01:19:13,716 --> 01:19:15,446
If there's a problem
with the SEC,

1362
01:19:15,618 --> 01:19:16,813
I'll handle it.

1363
01:19:16,986 --> 01:19:18,284
Now, where were you?

1364
01:19:20,823 --> 01:19:25,420
With Carl Gottlieb, the
CEO of the VSN Corporation,

1365
01:19:25,961 --> 01:19:27,725
discussing a possible takeover.

1366
01:19:27,896 --> 01:19:30,024
Here's his sworn affidavit.

1367
01:19:37,906 --> 01:19:41,035
Mr. Matlock, do you mind?

1368
01:19:51,320 --> 01:19:55,485
Well, whoever
killed Scott Aston,

1369
01:19:55,658 --> 01:19:58,253
we know it wasn't John
Robinson or Mark Reeves.

1370
01:19:58,427 --> 01:20:00,267
That's what you said
before about Roger Herman.

1371
01:20:00,429 --> 01:20:03,558
Well, we'll just have to prove that
he got Chuck Ratner to lie for him.

1372
01:20:03,732 --> 01:20:05,732
What if you can't prove
it? Ben, what if you can't?

1373
01:20:05,901 --> 01:20:07,893
The DA's case against
me is pretty circumstantial,

1374
01:20:08,070 --> 01:20:09,936
but it's, you know, some
strong circumstance.

1375
01:20:10,105 --> 01:20:13,371
They got the maid who overheard
me arguing with Scott Aston.

1376
01:20:13,542 --> 01:20:16,137
They got my hankie
stuffed in the toilet.

1377
01:20:16,312 --> 01:20:18,406
- The jury likes you.
- Really? Do they?

1378
01:20:18,580 --> 01:20:21,482
Yeah, I think Juror Number
8 has a crush on you.

1379
01:20:21,650 --> 01:20:24,085
- That's very funny.
- No, honestly.

1380
01:20:30,626 --> 01:20:32,993
Was Juror Number 8
the blond or the brunette?

1381
01:20:33,162 --> 01:20:34,653
Um...

1382
01:20:34,830 --> 01:20:36,492
What do people
need with all this stuff?

1383
01:20:36,665 --> 01:20:38,861
I mean, who uses bath
gel or shower caps?

1384
01:20:39,034 --> 01:20:40,714
When's the last time
you wore a shower cap?

1385
01:20:40,869 --> 01:20:42,735
Yeah. Heh.

1386
01:20:45,874 --> 01:20:48,469
How long was Scott
Aston in this room?

1387
01:20:48,644 --> 01:20:51,671
Oh, uh, since about
noon on the day he died.

1388
01:20:51,847 --> 01:20:55,147
They gave him a new room
when he got back from Florida.

1389
01:20:55,918 --> 01:20:58,319
Call Housekeeping and ask them

1390
01:20:58,487 --> 01:21:01,457
if a little sewing kit
comes in these baskets.

1391
01:21:01,623 --> 01:21:02,852
- Sewing kit?
- Yeah.

1392
01:21:03,025 --> 01:21:05,824
- Call them and ask them.
- Right.

1393
01:21:08,063 --> 01:21:09,623
I had drinks with
Mr. Herman that night,

1394
01:21:09,798 --> 01:21:12,996
but I left around 7.

1395
01:21:13,602 --> 01:21:18,404
Before, under oath, you
said you left around 8:30.

1396
01:21:18,707 --> 01:21:20,801
Yes, sir, I know, I...

1397
01:21:21,176 --> 01:21:23,008
Mr. Herman told me to.

1398
01:21:23,178 --> 01:21:25,374
He threatened to
cut off my pension.

1399
01:21:25,547 --> 01:21:30,986
Without it, I can't care
for my son, so I lied.

1400
01:21:31,653 --> 01:21:33,087
I see.

1401
01:21:33,255 --> 01:21:36,714
Uh, one more thing, do
you happen to remember

1402
01:21:36,892 --> 01:21:39,987
what Mr. Herman was
wearing that evening?

1403
01:21:40,162 --> 01:21:42,961
Oh, gosh, I... Um...

1404
01:21:44,099 --> 01:21:46,728
I think he was wearing
light-brown pants

1405
01:21:46,902 --> 01:21:49,098
and a yellow striped shirt.

1406
01:21:49,271 --> 01:21:51,797
Uh-huh. No jacket?

1407
01:21:51,974 --> 01:21:55,035
It was warm that night. I
wasn't wearing a jacket either.

1408
01:21:56,145 --> 01:21:58,046
No more questions.

1409
01:21:58,213 --> 01:22:01,377
I understand you've been
indicted for fraud, Mr. Herman.

1410
01:22:01,550 --> 01:22:03,018
I've been indicted, yes.

1411
01:22:03,185 --> 01:22:05,177
Well, I'm gonna ask
you some questions.

1412
01:22:05,354 --> 01:22:07,516
Some of it has to do with fraud.

1413
01:22:07,689 --> 01:22:09,885
Now, there's a large
cast of characters.

1414
01:22:10,058 --> 01:22:11,617
Like they used to
say about the movies,

1415
01:22:11,794 --> 01:22:13,353
cast of thousands.

1416
01:22:13,529 --> 01:22:16,624
So you think carefully
before you answer

1417
01:22:16,799 --> 01:22:18,995
so you don't, you
know, wind up in trouble.

1418
01:22:19,168 --> 01:22:20,431
You ready?

1419
01:22:22,237 --> 01:22:26,299
Was, uh, Cameron Ivers
interested in buying your business?

1420
01:22:26,708 --> 01:22:28,472
He never talked to me about it.

1421
01:22:28,644 --> 01:22:33,605
I hear that he was the kind of fellow
that, when he took over a company,

1422
01:22:33,782 --> 01:22:37,241
he went through that thing with
a fine-tooth comb. Is that right?

1423
01:22:37,953 --> 01:22:40,980
- I suppose.
- So if there'd been any fraud going on

1424
01:22:41,156 --> 01:22:43,421
at Ashfield Life, he'd
have found it, wouldn't he?

1425
01:22:43,592 --> 01:22:44,924
I wouldn't know.

1426
01:22:45,093 --> 01:22:47,426
Oh, come on, Mr. Herman.

1427
01:22:47,596 --> 01:22:49,565
I'm talking major fraud.

1428
01:22:49,731 --> 01:22:51,700
What if Mr. Ivers found out

1429
01:22:51,867 --> 01:22:56,703
that you were creating
fake life-insurance policies

1430
01:22:56,872 --> 01:22:59,398
and selling them to other
insurance companies?

1431
01:22:59,575 --> 01:23:02,374
And what if he found
out that you were creating

1432
01:23:02,544 --> 01:23:04,376
fake death certificates

1433
01:23:04,546 --> 01:23:07,141
and then collecting
all the death benefits

1434
01:23:07,316 --> 01:23:09,581
from these other
companies for yourself?

1435
01:23:11,253 --> 01:23:14,917
That's a scheme amounting to
hundreds of millions of dollars.

1436
01:23:15,090 --> 01:23:16,752
What if he found out about that?

1437
01:23:18,160 --> 01:23:20,720
I, uh... I refuse
to answer that.

1438
01:23:20,896 --> 01:23:23,764
I don't blame you. Heh.

1439
01:23:23,932 --> 01:23:26,333
Are you following all this?

1440
01:23:26,602 --> 01:23:28,935
If Craig Browning had
thrown Mr. Ivers' case,

1441
01:23:29,104 --> 01:23:31,073
well, Mr. Ivers
would've gone to jail,

1442
01:23:31,240 --> 01:23:33,175
and that probably
would've been the end of it.

1443
01:23:33,775 --> 01:23:35,141
But he didn't throw his case.

1444
01:23:36,245 --> 01:23:38,407
He didn't go to jail.

1445
01:23:38,881 --> 01:23:40,213
What he did,

1446
01:23:40,382 --> 01:23:44,080
Craig Browning got my
associate, Cliff Lewis,

1447
01:23:44,253 --> 01:23:47,121
to intercede for him
and go to a meeting

1448
01:23:47,456 --> 01:23:51,757
at a bar with this private investigator
about this blackmail business.

1449
01:23:52,728 --> 01:23:55,061
He met him. He had him followed.

1450
01:23:55,230 --> 01:23:59,998
He got pictures of Mr. Aston,
Mr. Robinson, Mr. Reeves.

1451
01:24:00,168 --> 01:24:04,128
He went up to Mr. Aston's
hotel room and confronted him.

1452
01:24:04,306 --> 01:24:08,141
He said, "You take my man
down, I'll take your man down."

1453
01:24:08,877 --> 01:24:11,437
Cliff was thrown
out of the room.

1454
01:24:12,214 --> 01:24:16,242
Sometime later,
about, oh, 10:00,

1455
01:24:16,618 --> 01:24:22,023
the maid that worked there at the
hotel discovered Mr. Aston's body.

1456
01:24:22,424 --> 01:24:23,619
There had been a fight.

1457
01:24:23,792 --> 01:24:26,261
He was dead, hit over the
head with a bottle of champagne.

1458
01:24:26,595 --> 01:24:28,894
Dinner was knocked
over, stuff all over the floor.

1459
01:24:29,064 --> 01:24:30,088
It was a mess.

1460
01:24:31,133 --> 01:24:35,798
And then she remembered Cliff

1461
01:24:36,438 --> 01:24:39,374
pounding on Mr. Aston's door,

1462
01:24:39,541 --> 01:24:41,806
shouting to be let in.

1463
01:24:42,611 --> 01:24:44,375
Then they found
Cliff's handkerchief.

1464
01:24:44,546 --> 01:24:47,209
Somebody would've tried
to flush it down the toilet.

1465
01:24:47,382 --> 01:24:48,782
The upshot of it

1466
01:24:48,951 --> 01:24:53,582
is the police charged Cliff
with Scott Aston's murder.

1467
01:24:54,723 --> 01:24:56,885
I know this man.

1468
01:24:57,426 --> 01:24:59,292
He didn't do it.

1469
01:25:00,228 --> 01:25:02,129
What he did,

1470
01:25:02,297 --> 01:25:05,199
he went the extra
mile for his client.

1471
01:25:05,367 --> 01:25:09,634
He respected
lawyer-client confidentiality,

1472
01:25:09,805 --> 01:25:11,899
and I respect him for that.

1473
01:25:12,674 --> 01:25:14,643
He didn't do it.

1474
01:25:19,848 --> 01:25:21,043
You did.

1475
01:25:25,220 --> 01:25:27,553
You're out of your mind.

1476
01:25:28,924 --> 01:25:31,917
Remember your
ex-employee, Chuck Ratner?

1477
01:25:33,028 --> 01:25:35,054
He called you, met
you at your club,

1478
01:25:35,230 --> 01:25:39,326
told you that Scott Aston was
about to find out about your fraud.

1479
01:25:39,501 --> 01:25:42,232
You knew you had to
do something, and now.

1480
01:25:42,404 --> 01:25:44,839
So you went up to his
room and did it, didn't you?

1481
01:25:45,841 --> 01:25:47,070
No.

1482
01:25:48,777 --> 01:25:51,406
Mr. Ratner just testified

1483
01:25:51,580 --> 01:25:53,981
that you bullied him
into perjuring himself

1484
01:25:54,149 --> 01:25:58,746
by saying that you were with
him at the time of the murder.

1485
01:25:59,554 --> 01:26:03,355
I did it because I knew the
truth would make me look bad.

1486
01:26:03,525 --> 01:26:04,720
What kind of truth?

1487
01:26:05,227 --> 01:26:06,456
I left the club,

1488
01:26:06,628 --> 01:26:08,961
and I walked around
for a couple of hours.

1489
01:26:09,131 --> 01:26:10,895
I needed time to think.

1490
01:26:11,066 --> 01:26:14,036
And when it became apparent
that I'd be better off with an alibi,

1491
01:26:14,202 --> 01:26:16,797
I asked Chuck to give me one.

1492
01:26:16,972 --> 01:26:20,374
Would you say his description
of what you were wearing

1493
01:26:20,542 --> 01:26:22,238
that evening was pretty correct?

1494
01:26:22,411 --> 01:26:24,380
I don't remember
what I was wearing.

1495
01:26:24,546 --> 01:26:28,813
Well, could you have
been wearing, uh, these?

1496
01:26:30,819 --> 01:26:34,449
- Yes, those are mine.
- Yeah, those are yours.

1497
01:26:34,623 --> 01:26:37,149
The police got them,
with a warrant, of course.

1498
01:26:37,859 --> 01:26:41,489
Cliff and I were looking
around the scene of the crime

1499
01:26:41,663 --> 01:26:44,792
trying to, you know,
see what we could find.

1500
01:26:45,801 --> 01:26:49,260
And what we couldn't find
were those little sewing kits

1501
01:26:49,438 --> 01:26:52,931
that Housekeeping
puts in all the rooms.

1502
01:26:53,542 --> 01:26:55,067
You know where I'm
heading, don't you?

1503
01:26:55,844 --> 01:26:58,006
No, no, no, I don't.

1504
01:26:58,180 --> 01:27:02,618
Well, like I said before,
there was a fight,

1505
01:27:04,953 --> 01:27:09,448
and this man, I'm sure, was
doing all he could do to save his life.

1506
01:27:09,624 --> 01:27:14,858
I bet grabbing at anything he
could, even the killer's shirt, huh?

1507
01:27:15,030 --> 01:27:17,192
Rich man couldn't
go out on the street

1508
01:27:17,365 --> 01:27:20,267
with his hair all messed
up like that after a fight,

1509
01:27:20,435 --> 01:27:23,428
with his shirt
hanging half off of him.

1510
01:27:23,605 --> 01:27:27,007
He couldn't go home to
his wife like that, could he?

1511
01:27:27,175 --> 01:27:29,007
Your Honor, this
is preposterous.

1512
01:27:29,177 --> 01:27:33,239
- Mr. Matlock.
- Yes, ma'am, we'll cut to the chase.

1513
01:27:33,415 --> 01:27:35,247
Now, there was a fight.

1514
01:27:35,417 --> 01:27:39,513
He was strong, but
you were younger.

1515
01:27:39,688 --> 01:27:42,157
And you won the fight.

1516
01:27:42,390 --> 01:27:44,052
And you killed him.

1517
01:27:44,226 --> 01:27:47,685
You hit him over the head
with this champagne bottle.

1518
01:27:47,929 --> 01:27:51,832
Then you grabbed the
handkerchief off the dresser,

1519
01:27:52,000 --> 01:27:55,300
Cliff's handkerchief, wiped
the blood off your hands.

1520
01:27:55,470 --> 01:27:58,929
Then you went in the bathroom,
tried to flush it down the toilet.

1521
01:27:59,107 --> 01:28:02,805
Then you saw the sewing kit,

1522
01:28:02,978 --> 01:28:05,743
and you used it
to sew the buttons

1523
01:28:05,914 --> 01:28:08,440
that had been torn
off back onto your shirt

1524
01:28:08,617 --> 01:28:10,518
before you went
outside, didn't you?

1525
01:28:11,720 --> 01:28:12,847
No.

1526
01:28:13,021 --> 01:28:14,284
Okay, here we have it.

1527
01:28:14,456 --> 01:28:18,450
When the police forensics
got done with this shirt,

1528
01:28:18,627 --> 01:28:21,722
they found that not
only was the thread

1529
01:28:21,897 --> 01:28:25,857
that were holding these three
buttons on the shirt different

1530
01:28:26,034 --> 01:28:28,333
from the thread holding
the other buttons,

1531
01:28:28,503 --> 01:28:32,099
but it was identical
in weight and dye lot

1532
01:28:32,874 --> 01:28:35,036
to the thread in
this little sewing kit.

1533
01:28:39,881 --> 01:28:42,874
You know, this is
really something.

1534
01:28:43,718 --> 01:28:45,710
Here's a man

1535
01:28:46,054 --> 01:28:49,786
who dealt with millions
of dollars every day,

1536
01:28:49,958 --> 01:28:54,328
a lot of it illegal, but
millions of dollars.

1537
01:28:55,463 --> 01:28:58,922
Often there's just
a little mistake,

1538
01:28:59,801 --> 01:29:03,465
and one piece of thread

1539
01:29:04,206 --> 01:29:05,230
worth, oh...

1540
01:29:05,407 --> 01:29:09,276
What, a penny, two at the most,

1541
01:29:11,112 --> 01:29:16,210
ties you to Scott Aston's death
for as long as you might live.

1542
01:29:22,724 --> 01:29:24,488
Thank you, Your
Honor, that's all.

1543
01:29:26,228 --> 01:29:30,863
<i>On a Sunday morn
Sat a maid forlorn</i>

1544
01:29:31,032 --> 01:29:35,470
<i>With her sweetheart by her side</i>

1545
01:29:35,637 --> 01:29:37,970
<i>Through the windowpane</i>

1546
01:29:38,139 --> 01:29:40,404
<i>She looked at the rain</i>

1547
01:29:40,575 --> 01:29:44,808
<i>"We must stay
home, Joe" She cried</i>

1548
01:29:44,980 --> 01:29:47,006
<i>She cried</i>

1549
01:29:47,482 --> 01:29:52,185
<i>There's a picnic too
At the old Point View</i>

1550
01:29:52,354 --> 01:29:59,325
<i>It's a shame it
rained today Today</i>

1551
01:29:59,594 --> 01:30:03,156
<i>Then the boy drew near</i>

1552
01:30:03,331 --> 01:30:07,826
<i>Kissed away each tear</i>

1553
01:30:08,003 --> 01:30:16,003
<i>And she heard him softly say</i>

1554
01:30:16,645 --> 01:30:20,639
<i>Wait till the sun shines, Nellie</i>

1555
01:30:20,815 --> 01:30:24,411
<i>When the clouds go drifting by</i>

1556
01:30:24,586 --> 01:30:25,884
<i>We will be happy...</i>

1557
01:30:26,054 --> 01:30:29,047
- They're so wonderful.
- They still got it.

1558
01:30:29,224 --> 01:30:32,490
Ben sounds a lot better with
them than he ever did by himself,

1559
01:30:32,661 --> 01:30:33,890
- that's for sure.
- Ha, ha!

1560
01:30:34,062 --> 01:30:36,759
You know, I can't believe
he still fits in the costume.

1561
01:30:36,932 --> 01:30:38,958
He had it let out.

1562
01:30:39,134 --> 01:30:42,969
- How do you know?
- Heard him talking to his tailor.

1563
01:30:43,438 --> 01:30:51,438
<i>Bye and bye Bye and
- Bye - Yes, bye and</i>

1564
01:31:00,255 --> 01:31:05,250
<i>Bye</i>

9999
00:00:0,500 --> 00:00:2,00
